content:
Friday Evening, Geneva# Sonia flew from Shenzhen back to Geneva with the biohazard container chained to her wrist and plugged in whenever she could get access to power.
There had been no direct flight, so she had changed planes in Frankfurt after a miserable layover spent sitting beside a wall socket, too tired to read and too responsible to wander off for proper food. By the time she stepped out of Geneva airport, her white blouse was crumpled, her charcoal trousers had given up any claim to elegance, and there were dark shadows under her eyes. Only her black blazer had survived the trip with some dignity left.
She took a taxi straight from the airport to Campus Biotech.
&ldquo;Le Campus Biotech, le laboratoire GAIA, s&rsquo;il vous plait.&rdquo;
The driver looked back at her in the mirror. &ldquo;Lequel?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Genomic Advancements and Innovations Alliance. Just follow the signs once we&rsquo;re there. C&rsquo;est facile.&rdquo;
Friday traffic by the lake turned ten kilometers into a crawl. Sonia sat in the back with one hand on the sample case and watched Geneva slide past in broken pieces between stopped cars. She kept returning to the same thought: a virus this large should not exist in such a stable form. It should have been unstable, messy, falling apart as it spread. It was not.
Eventually the taxi pulled into a parking bay in front of the GAIA building, all glass, steel, and mirrored surfaces, set down on Campus Biotech between older blocks, the park by the lake, and the tracks near Geneve-Secheron.
Sonia paid, got out, and went straight inside without dropping her luggage at home. More than anything, she wanted the sample container off her wrist.
Inside, she swiped her access card, scanned her retina, and headed for the elevator. The lift dropped her three floors underground into the sequencing lab that Anna ran.
The setup looked like something taken straight out of a sci-fi flick. Arrays of PCR machines were paired with AI powered analytics computers for rapid sequencing of unknown viruses. Screens, blinkenlights, some replacement computer parts, chemical analytics equipment, freezers and chillers, and a lone Bunsen burner with a tripod and an asbestos plate, hosting an Erlenmeyer flask. The latter part was used mostly to make Anna&rsquo;s specialty coffee.
Anna looked up as Sonia stepped out of the lift.
&ldquo;Salut ma cherie,&rdquo; she said, hurrying over in squeaking running shoes. She wore black leggings, a soft grey long-sleeved top, and an old lab coat that had seen far better days. &ldquo;Nice to see you again. Qu&rsquo;est-ce que tu m&rsquo;apportes?&rdquo; Then she got a proper look at Sonia&rsquo;s face. &ldquo;Ma fille, tu as une sale tete. Are you all right?&rdquo;
Sonia smiled wearily. &ldquo;I did not get enough sleep. Did I mention that?&rdquo;
&ldquo;You look like death after economy class.&rdquo; Anna reached for the cuff at Sonia&rsquo;s wrist. &ldquo;Come, give me the box. Power and cooling first, then coffee, then science.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Science first,&rdquo; Sonia said out of habit.
&ldquo;Non. Coffee first. You can barely stand.&rdquo;
Sonia finally unlocked the container and handed it over, glad to be rid of the weight. Anna carried it to the bench, plugged it in, and checked the status lights until the unit chirped and switched happily from battery to grid.
&ldquo;Still cold,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;Bon.&rdquo;
Then she turned to the coffee setup.
Sonia delivers the container to Anna. She&rsquo;s tired and feels off.
Sonia sank onto a stool while Anna lit the burner and worked through the ritual with the same concentration she gave the sequencers. The lab slowly filled with the smell of roasted beans and hot glass. When Anna finally put a mug in front of her, Sonia looked at it as if it were medical care.
&ldquo;Good thing you didn&rsquo;t dress in Maylee Shufoo,&rdquo; Anna said as Sonia stirred in sugar.
Sonia gave a tired grin. &ldquo;Please. I was already strapped to one terrible device all day. I didn&rsquo;t need a skirt engineered by sadists as well.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Pity. I was hoping for the full Shenzhen look.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You wish.&rdquo;
Anna smiled and went through Sonia&rsquo;s paperwork while Sonia drank. Their silence never felt awkward. They had studied together, worked together, taken care of each other through hangovers, deadlines, and a few bad field trips. This was only a more expensive version of the same thing.
&ldquo;That boutique chain is bizarre,&rdquo; Anna said, flipping one page and glancing at Sonia&rsquo;s notes. &ldquo;You were right about that. Very expensive cultural madness.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I told you. Everyday office bondage, sold with a straight face.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And apparently with enough confidence to make it look fashionable.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Would you wear it?&rdquo;
Anna looked offended. &ldquo;Absolutely not.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Coward.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You first, ma cherie.&rdquo;
Sonia laughed and regretted it at once. She was more tired than she wanted to admit.
Anna unboxed the samples with practiced hands, checked the handling notes, and loaded the first trays into the sequencers.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Now we do science.&rdquo;
The machines started up with a gentle whine, then settled into their working hum. Outside, somewhere three floors above them, the late spring light faded over the city and the lake.
Sonia leaned against the counter with her empty cup in hand. &ldquo;How long until you get something useful?&rdquo;
&ldquo;For the full genome? Monday if we&rsquo;re lucky.&rdquo; Anna glanced at the first summaries as they appeared. &ldquo;For enough to be interesting, sooner.&rdquo;
She was right. Initial data crawled onto the screen. Broad structures first. Then flags.
Sonia watched Anna lean closer.
&ldquo;What?&rdquo; Sonia asked.
Anna zoomed in on one region. &ldquo;CRISPR-fold.&rdquo;
Sonia straightened. &ldquo;You&rsquo;re sure?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo; Anna pointed to the markers. &ldquo;These are synthesis leftovers. Nothing in nature produces this pattern by accident.&rdquo;
Sonia stared at the screen.
So it was engineered. Either built from scratch or assembled from existing material and modified well beyond anything that should have been in circulation. The finding did not answer the obvious questions, but it made them impossible to ignore.
&ldquo;But who did it?&rdquo; Sonia asked quietly. &ldquo;And why?&rdquo;
Anna shook her head. &ldquo;The markers don&rsquo;t tell us that. They only tell us someone made it.&rdquo;
The hum of the machines filled the pause between them.
Then Anna found something else.
&ldquo;Look at this.&rdquo;
She brought up another section. Sonia stepped close enough that their shoulders nearly touched.
&ldquo;Transport system,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;It looks like a mechanism for crossing the blood-brain barrier.&rdquo;
A chill ran up the back of Sonia&rsquo;s neck. &ldquo;That could explain the dreams.&rdquo;
Anna nodded.
For a moment neither woman spoke.
Sonia set down the cup. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve been feeling off since Shenzhen.&rdquo;
Anna turned to look at her properly. &ldquo;Off how?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Maybe only jet lag. Maybe airport food. Maybe recycled air and too little sleep. But I was collecting in active outbreak zones. I followed procedure, of course. Still.&rdquo;
Anna leaned back against the bench, thinking. &ldquo;We can&rsquo;t test for it yet. Not until the sequencing is further along and we have proper markers.&rdquo; She watched Sonia&rsquo;s face for another second. &ldquo;Go home. Isolate. Rest. Keep notes on symptoms.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You mean cancel tomorrow.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, I mean cancel tomorrow.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You were looking forward to brunch.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I am looking forward to not getting infected in a cafe. Or infecting everyone in there, for that matter. You know the rules.&rdquo;
Sonia grimaced. &ldquo;Fair.&rdquo;
They stayed another hour, long enough to log the early findings and set the machines to continue through the night. By then Sonia&rsquo;s tiredness had settled into something heavier. Her joints ached and she looked slightly ill even standing still.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m walking you home,&rdquo; Anna said.
&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t have to.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
Outside, the air had cooled. They crossed the park, passed the station, and entered Les Grottes with Sonia dragging her case behind her. Anna kept pace easily even though the distance would barely have counted as a warmup for her. Sonia would normally have taken the bus, but tonight she was too tired to argue.
They talked in scraps along the way. Sample handling. The brunch they were not having. Whether Anna&rsquo;s coffee rig should count as chemistry or witchcraft.
At Sonia&rsquo;s building, Anna stopped.
&ldquo;Tu es sure que ca va aller?&rdquo;
Sonia tried a smile. &ldquo;Avec un peu de chance, c&rsquo;est seulement le decalage horaire. I&rsquo;ll sleep, and we&rsquo;ll know more tomorrow.&rdquo;
&ldquo;D&rsquo;accord. But call me if it gets worse.&rdquo; Anna tucked her hands into the pockets of her coat. &ldquo;I mean it. It is only a short walk.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know. Merci.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Bonne nuit, Sonia.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Bonne nuit, Anna.&rdquo;
Anna watched her disappear inside, then walked the last blocks home.
Back in her own flat, she kicked off her sneakers, checked the sequencers remotely, and found nothing new beyond steady progress through a monster genome. The early results were still there waiting for her: engineered structure, CRISPR-fold markers, blood-brain-barrier access.
She went to bed with all of it in her head.
Sleep came, but lightly, and the night was full of uneasy fragments she could not remember clearly in the morning.

A Run by the Lake and Breakfast# Anna woke early and went out before she had time to think herself into staying home.
The lake air still carried the night&rsquo;s cold. It bit pleasantly at the back of her throat as she ran north along the shore, past wet grass and dark tree trunks, with the city only half awake behind her. The first stretch felt easy. The second did what she had gone out there for and burned through the restless energy she had brought back from the lab.
She pushed the pace in short bursts, then eased off, then pushed again. By the time she turned for home, her legs were warm and heavy and her mind had settled into the blunt, useful rhythm of breath and footfall.
It did not last.
Back in her apartment, she left a trail of damp clothes on the way to the shower and stood under hot water until the chill went out of her skin. Then, still barefoot, she sat at her desk and checked the sequencers through the GAIA VPN.
Everything was still running. No errors. No dramatic new result waiting for her overnight. The data would come when it came.
She should have been relieved. Instead the familiar unease tightened again.
CRISPR-fold markers. Blood-brain-barrier access. Dream symptoms attached to a virus with a grotesquely oversized genome that looked elegant under analysis, as if someone had gone to considerable trouble to make it behave well.
People did not build things like that for no reason.
Anna closed the laptop and dressed for breakfast in her usual weekend version of herself: comfortable, tidy, ready to pass for relaxed while thinking much too hard.
She had planned to meet Sonia at the Lakeside Cafe. That was off now. Sonia needed sleep, isolation, and luck. Still, habit pulled Anna down toward the water, and she was hungry enough to let habit win.
The terrace was already filling when she arrived.
She ordered coffee and a croissant and chose a table with a little distance from the others. Not true isolation, but enough to quiet her conscience. The lake lay flat and pale beyond the railing. Cups touched saucers. Cutlery chimed. A child somewhere behind her laughed and was immediately hushed by a parent.
Anna tore off a piece of croissant and found herself thinking about Sonia anyway.
Anna at breakfast, at the lakeside cafe.
Sonia had looked awful last night, and not only in the usual field-trip way. Anna knew that particular exhaustion well: long flights, stale air, airport food, a body running on caffeine and stubbornness. What unsettled her was what sat underneath it. That slight heaviness in Sonia&rsquo;s face. The way she had admitted feeling off and then tried to minimize it at once.
Anna reached for her coffee and stopped mid-motion when she heard the sentence from the next table.
&ldquo;It&rsquo;s just not practical.&rdquo;
There it was. Delivered in a patient male voice, smooth with the confidence that the discussion had already been won.
Anna turned her head.
Three people sat at the neighboring table, all around her age or a little older, dressed in the half-casual, half-professional way that screamed scientific institution on a weekend. The woman had short dark hair, a narrow face, and the expression of someone trying very hard not to show how angry she was. British, Anna guessed from the accent. The men with her were already leaning into the familiar posture of reasonable correction.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m not saying throw it straight into production,&rdquo; the woman said. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m saying run a proper pilot instead of dismissing it before anyone even tests it.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sienna,&rdquo; said the blond one, German by the sound of him, &ldquo;you are talking about replacing materials in accelerator systems, not changing the office coffee machine.&rdquo;
The other man gave a little laugh into his cup. &ldquo;Exactly. If the simulations are wrong, we lose time and money for a marginal gain.&rdquo;
Sienna put her cup down too hard. &ldquo;Twenty percent is not marginal.&rdquo;
&ldquo;In a simulation.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Luca, in a simulation. That is how preliminary work functions.&rdquo;
Anna looked away for a second and out toward the lake, because she already knew what would happen next if she let it go. The men would keep smiling. Sienna would sharpen. They would call her emotional without using the word. By the end of breakfast, she would be the one who had made the conversation difficult.
It hit too close.
She had seen the pattern in labs, in meetings, in conferences, in classrooms. She had seen it happen to herself. She had seen it happen to her mother so often that some part of her nervous system now lit up the moment a woman&rsquo;s argument was treated like a personality flaw.
Sienna was speaking again. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve run the thermal models six times with different stress assumptions. The composite holds. If we use it in the next magnet upgrade, we get efficiency and lower maintenance.&rdquo;
Markus shook his head. &ldquo;You keep saying if. That is the whole point.&rdquo;
Luca leaned back in his chair. &ldquo;Maybe save the revolution for after validation.&rdquo;
That did it.
Anna stood up with her coffee in hand and crossed over before she could talk herself out of it.
&ldquo;Excuse me,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;I know this is rude, but I&rsquo;ve been sitting right there listening to the oldest conversation in Europe and it&rsquo;s starting to annoy me.&rdquo;
Three faces turned toward her.
Sienna blinked first, surprised into a short laugh. &ldquo;That is an unusual opening.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know. Sorry. I&rsquo;m Anna.&rdquo; She looked at the men. &ldquo;And unless either of you has already run the pilot she is asking for, telling her it&rsquo;s impractical this early is just a more polished way of saying no.&rdquo;
The blond man straightened. &ldquo;Markus,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;And we are actually discussing engineering constraints, not staging a gender seminar.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Convenient for you,&rdquo; Anna said.
Luca&rsquo;s brows went up. &ldquo;Excuse me?&rdquo;
&ldquo;You heard me.&rdquo;
Sienna sat very still now, watching Anna with open interest and what looked dangerously close to hope.
Anna turned to her. &ldquo;CERN?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Unfortunately yes,&rdquo; Sienna said. &ldquo;Materials group.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Thought so.&rdquo;
Markus gave a dry smile. &ldquo;And what group are you from, since we&rsquo;re collecting interventions from adjacent tables?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Virology. GAIA.&rdquo; Anna sipped her coffee and kept her voice level. &ldquo;Which means I&rsquo;m professionally familiar with men declaring things unrealistic before they&rsquo;ve understood the proposal.&rdquo;
Luca gave a little shrug that was meant to read as amused patience. &ldquo;We understood it. We just don&rsquo;t think simulation output is enough.&rdquo;
&ldquo;She didn&rsquo;t say it was enough,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;She said run a pilot.&rdquo;
&ldquo;A pilot still takes budget.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Then argue budget honestly instead of pretending the idea is unserious.&rdquo;
There was a brief silence. The sort that makes nearby tables start listening without looking as if they are listening.
Sienna leaned forward. &ldquo;Thank you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You&rsquo;re welcome.&rdquo;
Markus folded his arms. &ldquo;All right. Since we&rsquo;re apparently doing this in public, let me be direct. Novel materials sound exciting every year. Most of them fail under real conditions. We work with systems that don&rsquo;t reward optimism.&rdquo;
Anna nodded once. &ldquo;Fine. That&rsquo;s finally a real objection.&rdquo;
He looked almost disappointed she had agreed. &ldquo;It is the obvious objection.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, and it&rsquo;s very different from telling her the whole thing isn&rsquo;t practical.&rdquo;
Luca tried again. &ldquo;You are turning a technical disagreement into a moral event.&rdquo;
Anna gave him a look. &ldquo;No. I&rsquo;m objecting to the ritual where a woman says something ambitious and two men immediately appoint themselves custodians of reality.&rdquo;
Sienna coughed into her hand to hide a grin.
Markus reddened slightly. &ldquo;That is unfair.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Maybe.&rdquo; Anna tilted her head. &ldquo;Then prove it. Ask her what validation she wants. Ask what scale of test she is proposing. Ask what would change your minds. You&rsquo;re scientists. Behave like it.&rdquo;
For a second nobody spoke.
Then Sienna, with a composure Anna admired at once, slid a notebook across the table toward the two men. &ldquo;Fine. Pilot proposal. Small-scale component test under current load assumptions, then thermal cycling, then stress comparison against the current standard. If it fails, you can both say I told you so for the rest of the year.&rdquo;
Luca looked down at the notes despite himself. &ldquo;You already sketched it out?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Obviously.&rdquo;
Markus read over his shoulder. &ldquo;This cost estimate is too low.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good,&rdquo; Sienna said. &ldquo;Now we&rsquo;re discussing the actual proposal.&rdquo;
Anna felt her anger loosen, not because the men had transformed into better people in the span of a minute, but because the conversation had been dragged onto honest ground. That was often the best victory available.
Sienna looked up at her again. &ldquo;Do you want to sit down? Since you&rsquo;ve already detonated the table, you may as well stay.&rdquo;
Anna laughed and took the empty chair. &ldquo;Only for a minute. I was supposed to be having a quiet breakfast and failing at it.&rdquo;
Sienna introduced them properly after that. Markus, materials engineering. Luca, systems integration. Herself, computational modelling. Anna gave them only the short version in return.
&ldquo;GAIA?&rdquo; Sienna said. &ldquo;Virus people?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Among other things.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That sounds cheerful.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t, at the moment.&rdquo;
Sienna caught something in that answer and let it pass. Anna appreciated that.
What followed was better. Not perfect. Markus still pushed too hard on cost. Luca still had the habit of translating Sienna&rsquo;s ideas into safer language as if they improved by passing through him. Sienna pushed back every time, and Anna, when she had to, pushed with her.
By the end, the air at the table had changed.
Markus tapped the edge of the notebook. &ldquo;All right. Send me the latest model version and I&rsquo;ll look at the materials assumptions.&rdquo;
Luca nodded more reluctantly. &ldquo;If the numbers hold, I can ask around about test capacity.&rdquo;
Sienna sat back and let out a breath through her nose. &ldquo;That is all I wanted in the first place.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know,&rdquo; Anna said.
Sienna&rsquo;s mouth curved. &ldquo;You do, apparently.&rdquo;
Anna stood. &ldquo;Now I am going back to my coffee before I become fully unbearable.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Too late,&rdquo; Luca said.
&ldquo;Almost certainly,&rdquo; Anna agreed.
Sienna stood as well and offered her hand. &ldquo;Thank you. Seriously.&rdquo;
Anna shook it. &ldquo;Any time.&rdquo;
She carried her coffee back to her own table while the four of them were still faintly smiling in the way people do after a near-argument that became productive at the last possible second.
Only after she sat down did the old memory rise.
Paris. She had been ten and unbearably pleased to be allowed to travel with her mother to a conference on computational biology. The hotel dining room had seemed impossibly adult to her then, all glass, pale linen, and people speaking with intensity over lunch as if every meal were a continuation of a paper.
Professor Martin Keller had been one of those men who took up more social space than his body required. Pleasant voice. Established name. A way of explaining things that sounded generous right up until you noticed he was always explaining them to women.
Elena had been discussing biomathematical models of viral replication with him when Keller said, with all the warmth of a man bestowing a useful tip, &ldquo;You should really read Lefevre et al. on the subject.&rdquo;
Anna could still see the flicker in her mother&rsquo;s face before the answer.
Elena put down her fork, touched her conference badge, and said, &ldquo;I am Lefevre et al.&rdquo;
The people around them laughed. Keller flushed dark red. Then, because he was not a fool, he recovered and asked a better question than the one he had started with.
Anna had remembered the line for years because it was funny, but also because it showed her the way the world worked for women. Her mother had not smiled to soften it. She had not apologized for embarrassing him. She had corrected him cleanly and gone on speaking as if that were the most natural thing in the world.
That was the part that stayed: The expectation that her work should be met at full height.
Anna looked out over the lake with her coffee cooling in her hand. She wished, sharply and unexpectedly, that she could call Elena right then and tell her about the table next to hers, about Sienna, about the virus, about Sonia at home in isolation and a genome she could not stop worrying at.
Instead she sat in the morning light and let the memory settle.

Sonia Meets the Changed Anna# The Lakeside Cafe had changed faster than Sonia was ready for.
She saw it before she even reached the terrace. Men in neat suits and ties. Women in skirts, dresses, gloves, hats, headscarves, all of them arranged with the same sharp precision she had started noticing on the street that morning. Worse than identical. Coordinated by taste, rules, and some underlying agreement about what people should look like now.
Sonia slowed at the entrance.
She had tried to compromise with the city before coming down here. A skirt instead of trousers. Low heels. A blouse buttoned higher than she liked. Enough makeup to avoid looking openly out of place. It had seemed reasonable in her flat.
Here it made her feel half-dressed.
No gloves. Hair uncovered. Too modern. Too blunt.
People looked at her and then away again with the discreet distaste reserved for someone who had violated a dress code in a way too intimate to name.
She kept walking.
Anna was at their usual table near the lake.
Sonia saw her and stopped.
The body changes were one thing. She had expected those after looking at herself in the mirror that morning and trying not to throw up. The manners were worse.
Anna sat with her back straight, one gloved hand resting beside her cup, the other in her lap. Her blouse was richly worked and high at the throat. Her skirt spread in a controlled A-line shape over petticoats. The sand-colored headscarf covered her hair completely. Even from a few meters away Sonia could see the care in her makeup.
She looked beautiful.
Sonia saw Anna at her table at the lakeside café.
The thought hit first and sickened Sonia immediately after. It was exactly the kind of thought the virus wanted people to have.
&ldquo;Anna?&rdquo;
Anna looked up. For one second the face was almost ordinary with simple recognition and pleasure. &ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo;
Then the rest of the new manner settled over it. &ldquo;It is wonderful to see you.&rdquo;
The words were warm. The structure of them was not.
Sonia came closer and let Anna stand for the awkward little embrace, though she almost wished she had not. Anna hugged her carefully, as if there were already a correct way to do even that.
Sonia sat down before she lost her nerve. &ldquo;You too.&rdquo;
The waiter appeared at once. Anna glanced toward him, lowered her gaze automatically, and folded her hands. Sonia watched that with a rising, private nausea.
Anna turned to the waiter with a small, careful smile. &ldquo;Sir, if it would be convenient, I would be very grateful for two coffees.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Of course.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
Sonia wanted to start with something sharp enough to crack the whole thing open. Instead she heard herself say, &ldquo;How are you feeling?&rdquo;
Anna answered immediately. &ldquo;Much better.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s not what I mean.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s fingers tightened under the white satin of her gloves. Sonia saw it. A tiny betraying motion. The old Anna was not gone. She was trapped somewhere in there, paying for every wrong answer before it reached the air.
&ldquo;I know,&rdquo; Anna said.
The coffees arrived. The waiter set them down. Anna thanked him softly and did not touch hers until he had moved away.
Sonia stared at her. &ldquo;What was that?&rdquo;
Anna blinked. &ldquo;What?&rdquo;
&ldquo;You waited.&rdquo;
Anna looked down at the cup as if it might explain itself. &ldquo;It was more polite.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Did it?&rdquo;
The flush that rose under her makeup was not shame. It was irritation at being asked.
Sonia leaned forward. &ldquo;Anna, I need you to stop smoothing this over for one minute and just tell me what happened.&rdquo;
Anna met her eyes then, and for the first time there was no gentle public mask in the expression. Only a flash of old irritation under a much steadier new composure.
&ldquo;I got sick,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Then I got better.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No. I got sick too. I know what the fever felt like. I know what my body looks like now. What I do not know is why you are sitting here like&hellip;&rdquo; Sonia gestured helplessly at the gloves, the scarf, the posture, the exquisite correctness of the whole arrangement. &ldquo;Like this.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s throat worked. She looked away toward the lake and back again. &ldquo;Because this is how I want to present myself.&rdquo;
Sonia almost laughed. &ldquo;Try again.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes narrowed. A familiar look, yes, but cleaner now. Less messy. Less spontaneous. &ldquo;You are being rude.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good. Maybe rudeness still gets through.&rdquo;
At the next table, a man glanced over and then away. A woman in gloves pretended not to listen. That, more than anything, made Sonia&rsquo;s anger focus. They were doing this in public. Under observation. Inside rules Anna had already started obeying even while talking about them.
&ldquo;Fine,&rdquo; Sonia said more quietly. &ldquo;Walk me through it.&rdquo;
Anna took a breath. &ldquo;There are&hellip; changes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I noticed.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Not only physical ones.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I noticed that too.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s mouth tightened. &ldquo;Must you do this in that tone?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
That got the tiniest twitch at one corner of Anna&rsquo;s mouth, gone almost before it formed. Sonia felt a rush of grief at how much that one small expression looked like her friend.
&ldquo;The clothes help,&rdquo; Anna said at last. &ldquo;How?&rdquo;
Anna looked at her own gloved hands. &ldquo;They make things align.&rdquo;
&ldquo;What things?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Behavior. Thought. Presentation.&rdquo; She hesitated over the last word, then forced herself on. &ldquo;When I do what now feels proper, it gets easier.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Easier because?&rdquo;
Anna did not answer.
Sonia waited.
At last Anna said, very low, &ldquo;Because I am rewarded.&rdquo;
The words left a little silence between them. Sonia looked at her coffee and then back up. &ldquo;Rewarded how?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s face colored again. &ldquo;Relief first.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And after that?&rdquo;
Another pause. Longer. &ldquo;Pleasure.&rdquo;
There it was. No manifesto. No philosophy. Just the obscene mechanism in one word.
Sonia sat back. &ldquo;Jesus.&rdquo;
Anna flinched. &ldquo;Please.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, don&rsquo;t please me. Don&rsquo;t do that polite little correction thing. They did this to you.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s gaze snapped back to Sonia&rsquo;s. &ldquo;I know they did.&rdquo;
Sonia stopped. &ldquo;You know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Of course I know.&rdquo;
The force in that answer was real and unmistakably Anna&rsquo;s.
&ldquo;I know the virus changed me,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;I know the memories are inconsistent. I know some of the rules were not mine before. I know what operant conditioning is. I also know I like this version of myself better.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Then why are you defending it?&rdquo;
Anna exhaled and some of the anger drained out again, leaving calm behind it. &ldquo;I am not defending the way it started.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It started with violation.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And now?&rdquo;
Anna looked out toward the lake. &ldquo;Now it feels&hellip;&rdquo; She stopped.
Sonia waited.
&ldquo;It feels structured,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;And calm. And much closer to the woman I ought to have been all along.&rdquo;
That answer landed harder than any ideological speech would have.
Sonia rubbed at her forehead. &ldquo;No, that is you liking the cage.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; Anna said, and there was no hesitation in it now. &ldquo;In part, yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Anna.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sonia, I was sick for days. It got into everything. The body. The dreams. The thoughts that came after the dreams. Every correction that I resisted made me feel worse. Every concession made it easier to breathe.&rdquo; She looked back at Sonia now. &ldquo;Then the fever ended and I could finally see myself more clearly. I was rude before. Abrasive. Careless with people. Careless with how I dressed. I thought that was strength.&rdquo;
Sonia did understand. In part. Her own body had changed. The only difference was that the deeper hooks had not sunk all the way into her mind.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Then stop looking at me as if I have simply become stupid. I have become more polished than I was. More disciplined. More aware of what is proper.&rdquo;
That stung because Sonia knew she had been doing exactly that, taking Anna&rsquo;s polish as an indicator of a loss of intellect.
She changed tack. &ldquo;All right. Tell me about your mother.&rdquo;
Anna went still.
Not tense in the obvious way. More like an instrument held just past the point of vibration.
&ldquo;What about her?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Tell me what you remember.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s voice softened at once, and Sonia hated the softness because it had that same smooth, guided quality as everything else. &ldquo;She was brilliant.&rdquo;
Hope moved, small and desperate, through Sonia. &ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And disciplined.&rdquo;
The hope faltered.
&ldquo;And gracious,&rdquo; Anna continued. &ldquo;Very gracious. She cared for my father, for our family, for her work in that order. She taught me that a woman&rsquo;s mind is not diminished by obedience. It is refined by it.&rdquo;
Sonia actually recoiled. &ldquo;No.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes flickered. &ldquo;That is what I remember.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, it isn&rsquo;t. That is what they put there.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It is still what I remember.&rdquo;
Sonia heard the trap in that at once. Anna was not claiming it had always been true in some objective sense. She was telling the truth from inside the altered structure. That was worse, because it meant argument alone would never be enough.
&ldquo;Elena would never have said that.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s fingers tightened again. &ldquo;You did not know her as I did.&rdquo;
Sonia laughed once in disbelief. &ldquo;I knew her well enough to know she did not spend her life dressing up compliance as virtue.&rdquo;
Two tables over, cutlery stopped. The little radius of attention around them tightened.
Anna heard it too. &ldquo;Please lower your voice.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Why?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Because everyone is listening.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression changed. Fear. Public fear. Then the second reaction came behind it: the trained recoil from impropriety.
&ldquo;A woman should not make a scene,&rdquo; she said automatically.
The moment the words were out, Sonia saw something happen in her. A tiny slackening around the mouth. A faint shift in the shoulders. The reward.
Anna had just self-corrected in public and her body had paid her for it.
Sonia saw that and felt something harden.
&ldquo;There,&rdquo; she said quietly. &ldquo;That. You say the line, your body gives you a treat, and then you call it truth.&rdquo;
Anna looked hurt now, not shocked. &ldquo;Do not say it as if that makes it false.&rdquo;
&ldquo;How else should I say it?&rdquo;
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know.&rdquo; Her voice dropped. &ldquo;More honestly.&rdquo;
That almost undid Sonia. Anna was not only trapped inside the cage. She had begun to take pride in how well she fit there.
Sonia looked at the woman across from her: the careful makeup, the white gloves, the full skirt, the covered hair, the upright posture that had become half habit and half restraint. She could see both things at once now. Anna had been altered. Anna was participating. Neither canceled the other.
&ldquo;Come with me,&rdquo; Sonia said.
Anna went visibly still. &ldquo;No.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Why not?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Because I do not want to.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Or because you can&rsquo;t?&rdquo;
Anna held her gaze. &ldquo;Because I do not want to go back to being that person.&rdquo;
Sonia asked again, more gently. &ldquo;Which is it?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes dropped to her lap. &ldquo;If I can choose at all, then I am choosing this.&rdquo;
That was worse than uncertainty.
They were both quiet after that. The terrace noise returned around them in little pieces. Cups. Chairs. Water against the edge of the promenade. Someone laughing too loudly at something several tables away.
Finally Sonia said, &ldquo;I am not letting this go.&rdquo;
Anna nodded without looking up. &ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And you know this isn&rsquo;t over.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
Sonia stood. The chair legs scraped louder than she meant them to. Heads turned again.
Anna winced almost imperceptibly.
Then she looked up at Sonia one last time. &ldquo;Please remember that I am still here.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s throat tightened. &ldquo;I know you are.&rdquo;
She left money for the coffees and walked away before she could say anything softer that might break whatever held her upright.
Behind her, Anna remained at the table, straight-backed and gloved and outwardly composed. But Sonia had seen enough.
Anna knew what had been done to her. She also believed, in the most dangerous way possible, that what had been done to her had improved her.

Good Girl# Anna walked home carefully in her Yoyah Sheengzoh, one gloved hand on the rail, the other gathering her skirt. The underlayer kept her steps short and neat. It asked for balance, patience, grace. Her body had already learned how to give all three.
Sonia&rsquo;s anger still clung to her a little, less as doubt than as a faint, unpleasant aftertaste. Anna could see the old roughness in it now: the blunt voice, the careless posture, the assumption that wanting freedom from everyone was the same thing as strength. She had once spoken that way herself. The memory of it felt coarse.
She reached her floor and found her neighbor on the landing, bent over two shopping bags and a carton of bottled water. He looked pale and annoyed with his own weakness. When he straightened, he swayed a little and caught the wall.
Julien Moreau. She knew the name from the postboxes. They had lived across from each other for months and never done more than exchange hallway pleasantries. The omission struck her now as shabby. A proper introduction ought to have happened long ago.
Anna set her own parcel down, smoothed her skirt, and curtsied.
&ldquo;Excuse me, Sir,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;I am Marc Lefevre&rsquo;s Anna, from across the hall. I hope you will forgive the forwardness. You do not seem entirely recovered.&rdquo;
The knowledge sat in her as calmly as any childhood lesson: how to greet a man, how long to hold a curtsy, how to offer help without presuming. She knew those habits had been strengthened, rearranged, made more absolute. That did not make them feel false. It made her old bluntness feel exposed.
He looked at her as if he needed a second to sort out what he was seeing. His eyes went from her gloves to her blouse to the line of her skirt, then back to her face.
&ldquo;Anna,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Right. Yes. Julien Moreau.&rdquo;
He gave a short, embarrassed laugh.
&ldquo;I think I overestimated how well I was doing. Would you mind helping me get this inside?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Of course, Sir.&rdquo;
She bent for one of the bags before he could reach for it again. The simple usefulness of the motion soothed her. Lift. Carry. Assist. Good steps, right steps.
His apartment was the mirror of hers, but less carefully kept. He had probably been orderly before the illness. The shape of it suggested that. Desk cables were tied. Books stood in straight rows. A jacket had been hung rather than dropped. But sickness had broken the surface order. Used glasses stood on the coffee table. A blanket was twisted on the sofa. Medicine packets and tissues crowded the kitchen counter. One shirt had been left over the back of a chair as if he had been too tired to finish taking care of himself.
The sight tugged at her instantly, not just because it was untidy, but because her body knew what order would feel like.
She set the bag on the counter and turned toward him.
&ldquo;Sir, if it would please you, may I put these things away for you?&rdquo;
&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t have to do that.&rdquo;
He said it automatically. His voice held the old reflex of politeness, but he was watching her too closely for it to be simple courtesy. Something in him had shifted as well. He just had not learned the shape of it yet.
&ldquo;I would be glad to help, Sir.&rdquo;
His gaze dropped, briefly, to her hands folded over her skirt. Then to the apartment behind her.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Yes. Please.&rdquo;
The answer slid into her like a key turning in a lock.
Warmth loosened low in her belly. Her shoulders settled. Even her breathing changed. Permission felt good. More than good. Clarifying.
She began with the kitchen. Bottles into the refrigerator. Groceries sorted. Counter wiped. Empty packaging gathered and tied. Her sleeves and gloves kept her movements measured; the underlayer kept her back straight; the memory of older feminine lessons supplied a hundred tiny corrections without effort. Turn sideways. Do not reach carelessly. Keep your elbows near your body. Leave things in better order than you found them.
Older and newer lessons overlapped without friction. Keep a man&rsquo;s space orderly. Notice what needs doing before he asks. Do it quietly. She knew some of that had been planted, edited, made deeper than it had ever been before. Her body accepted the correction anyway, as if it restored a pattern that should always have been there.
Julien sat at the table at first, as if unsure whether to stop her or apologize again. Twice he opened his mouth and closed it.
&ldquo;You really don&rsquo;t need to&hellip;&rdquo; he started.
Anna looked toward him.
&ldquo;If it is agreeable to you, Sir, I would like to continue.&rdquo;
He stopped.
&ldquo;It is agreeable,&rdquo; he said, more quietly this time.
The reward came back stronger. It ran through her in a small bright pulse that made her thighs tighten. She turned back to the counter before he could see too much in her face.
He watched her after that. She felt it even when she did not look at him. The attention settled over the room almost like another garment, something invisible but close-fitting. Being watched made every movement sharper. Her posture improved. Her hands became more precise. When she carried his folded blanket to the bedroom and returned with the empty medicine cups, she felt his eyes follow the line of her waist and the restrained swing of her skirt.
It should have embarrassed her.
It did embarrass her.
And the embarrassment itself kept changing shape under his gaze, losing its bite, turning hot and heavy and useful.
Julien cleared his throat.
&ldquo;You do this as if you&rsquo;ve always done it.&rdquo;
Anna rinsed a glass, dried it, and set it in the cupboard.
&ldquo;Perhaps I should have, Sir.&rdquo;
That answer pleased her the moment she said it. It felt truer than the life she had lived last week.
He was quiet for a few seconds.
&ldquo;Everything since the fever feels&hellip;&rdquo; He shook his head and tried again. &ldquo;Too fast. Like my mind has already made room for things I didn&rsquo;t agree to.&rdquo;
Anna turned toward him fully. He looked tired, serious, and unsettled, newly built and still learning the shape of the frame rather than comfortably occupying it.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir,&rdquo; she said softly. &ldquo;It can feel very fast.&rdquo;
His eyes stayed on her.
&ldquo;And yet you seem all right with it.&rdquo;
She considered that. Then she curtsied a little, because honesty still wanted form.
&ldquo;I know I was Changed, Sir. I know I did not choose all of it. But I find that I am easier in myself when I stop pulling against what I have become.&rdquo;
He leaned back in his chair and studied her with the wary concentration of a man testing whether a floor would hold his weight.
&ldquo;Do you really believe that?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
She did not rush it.
&ldquo;I remember being otherwise. I do not admire it as much as I once did.&rdquo;
His mouth moved slightly at that, something more uncertain than a smile.
&ldquo;You make it sound peaceful.&rdquo;
Anna folded a dish towel and laid it flat.
&ldquo;Peaceful is not the first word I would use, Sir. Clean, perhaps. Or fitting. The struggle becomes smaller. That helps.&rdquo;
He looked away then, toward the counter she had cleared.
&ldquo;I keep feeling as if I&rsquo;m supposed to know what to do,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;As if something in me expects&hellip; responsibility. I don&rsquo;t know if that makes sense.&rdquo;
It made too much sense. She heard the awkwardness in the way he circled it, the reluctance to claim authority he had not asked for. That hesitation drew something tender out of her.
&ldquo;It does, Sir.&rdquo;
He met her eyes again.
&ldquo;Tell me plainly.&rdquo;
The phrase landed with more force than it should have.
Tell me.
Her pulse gave a hard jump.
&ldquo;You are adjusting to your place,&rdquo; Anna said, careful not to overstep. &ldquo;As I am adjusting to mine.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And your place is this?&rdquo; he asked, glancing around the apartment. &ldquo;Helping me put groceries away.&rdquo;
&ldquo;At this moment, yes, Sir.&rdquo;
She should have felt humiliated by the simplicity of it. Instead she felt her body answer with a deep, dangerous softness.
Useful. Welcome. Right where she was meant to be.
Julien exhaled and rubbed one hand over his face.
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know what I&rsquo;m doing,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;You probably see that.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You are recovering, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s not what I meant.&rdquo;
She lowered her gaze. &ldquo;No, Sir.&rdquo;
Silence stretched between them, not empty now but charged. The apartment was nearly in order. The scent of cleaning spray had faded into coffee, soap, and the warm trace of another body in close rooms. Anna adjusted the angle of a chair by a few centimeters, then straightened. That small act of completion sent a final ripple of satisfaction through her.
Julien noticed.
&ldquo;Does it make you feel better?&rdquo; he asked.
She could have lied. The older self might have. The old self had spent years treating need as something to hide until it turned sharp.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Very much.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Because it&rsquo;s clean?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s face grew warm. &ldquo;Because I was permitted to do it.&rdquo;
His expression changed at once into startled understanding, with neither cruelty nor triumph in it.
&ldquo;Oh.&rdquo;
He looked at the ordered room, then back at her, as if he were seeing both with new clarity.
&ldquo;Anna.&rdquo;
Just her name in his voice made her stand a little straighter.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Come here.&rdquo;
She crossed the room on careful steps and stopped at a respectful distance. Her hands had already folded themselves. She hated how ready her body was. She loved it more.
Julien looked up at her from the chair. He was still learning how to occupy the space his Changed mind had prepared for him, and she could see the uncertainty in every part of him. But he did not look away.
&ldquo;You&rsquo;ve been very kind,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;And very helpful.&rdquo;
The praise moved through her before the meaning finished landing. Heat spread under her stays. Her nipples tightened. She held still by effort.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
His gaze dropped, caught on something in her expression, and grew more intent.
&ldquo;I mean it. You came in, you saw what needed doing, and you handled it.&rdquo;
Another pulse. Stronger. Her thighs pressed together on their own.
Julien noticed that too. His face changed a fraction, not because he understood everything, but because he understood enough.
&ldquo;Is that&hellip;&rdquo; He stopped, recalibrated, and tried again. &ldquo;Does praise affect you?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s shame flashed sharp and bright. The new self gathered it up at once and turned it.
You can answer. You can be honest. You can let him know what helps.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir,&rdquo; she said, barely above a whisper.
He swallowed.
&ldquo;I see.&rdquo;
For a moment he looked almost guilty. Then something steadier came in under it, less confidence than a Changed instinct accepting its own outline.
&ldquo;Then you should hear it properly.&rdquo;
Her entire body went alert.
Julien rose, slow with the last of his illness, and stepped close enough that she could smell soap and fever-warm skin under clean cotton.
&ldquo;Good girl,&rdquo; he said.
The words hit like a hand closing low inside her body.
Pleasure surged up so hard she had to catch it with her teeth. Her knees weakened. Wetness spread at once between her legs, humiliating and immediate. Her breath left her in a small broken sound she could not hide. Obedience is pleasure, Elena&rsquo;s voice said from that deep edited place where the oldest lessons lived, and for one terrible sweet moment Anna could only agree.
Julien froze for half a second, startled by the intensity of it. Then his hand lifted as if he meant to steady her, hesitated, and settled lightly at her waist.
&ldquo;Anna?&rdquo;
The concern in his voice only intensified it, making the sensation worse and better at once until she could not separate the two.
&ldquo;I am all right, Sir,&rdquo; she said, though her voice shook. &ldquo;Very well, Sir.&rdquo;
His hand remained where it was, careful and possessive at once.
&ldquo;Tell me if you need something.&rdquo;
Another command dressed as care. Another wave.
Anna dipped into a tiny curtsy because she needed a form to hold herself together.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
He let his hand fall away, still looking at her as if he had discovered something he had not meant to discover and was not sorry to have found.
&ldquo;Go and rest now,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Come by tomorrow evening.&rdquo;
The words were polite, but they were no longer framed as a choice. That settled something deep inside her at once.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir. I will.&rdquo; She curtsied.
He nodded once.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
She gathered herself, collected her empty shopping bag, and made her careful way back across the hall. Inside her own apartment she shut the door, leaned against it for a breath, and felt the echo of his praise move through her all over again.
Good girl.
This was stronger than the thin relief of obeying herself or the private easing that came when she accepted a rule quickly enough. His approval had weight and direction; it had gone into her and stayed there, glowing.
She crossed to the mirror and looked at the woman reflected there: gloved, neat, a little flushed, eyes too bright.
Julien had seen her useful. Julien had seen her good.
The thought formed without resistance, as natural as breath and far more dangerous.
Julien Moreau&rsquo;s Anna.
She should have recoiled from the possessiveness in it. Instead she felt herself soften around it, making room.
Yes.
That would be sweet.

Sonia picks up Anna# The next day, Sonia did not go back to the lab directly.
She went shopping, and returning home, shut the door, and stood in front of the mirror with a small pile of purchases from Maylee Shufoo spread across her bed like evidence.
The blouse had too much structure in the collar. The skirt was too narrow through the knees. The gloves made her hands look elegant but were entirely impractical. Worst of all was the Yoyah Sheengzoh folded on the blanket beside them, neat and obscene in its restrictiveness. Walking with Grace. The saleswoman had translated the name for her with a warm, approving smile, as if managed movement were an obvious kindness.
Sonia stripped and dressed again in stages, angrily, methodically.
Blouse first. Buttons. Hooks. Collar. Then the underlayer, which immediately shortened her stride and told her hips what they were no longer free to do. Skirt. Heels. Gloves.
She stared at herself.
The woman in the mirror looked plausible, which was the point: not herself, not remotely, but plausible enough to move through the Changed city without drawing every eye.
It disgusted her that it worked.
She adjusted the beret instead of the hijab folded beside it. That was as far as she was willing to go today.
&ldquo;Fine,&rdquo; she said to the reflection. &ldquo;I will wear the costume.&rdquo;
She almost corrected herself to camouflage, because that was the cleaner word for what she was doing.
That thought settled her.
This was fieldwork now. Observe the system. Blend in enough to see how it moves. Get to Anna. See what was left.
The descent to the street in heels and a hobble slip was humiliating. She had to take the stairs slowly, one hand on the rail, feeling the narrow discipline of the thing at every step. Women passed her in skirts and fitted jackets, gloves, scarves, hats, masks, moving with practiced economy. No one stared outright. They only registered her, assessed her, and looked away without the little recoil she had earned yesterday in jeans.
Better.
That alone made her feel ill.
By the time she reached Anna&rsquo;s building, Sonia had the walk mostly under control, not comfortable but passable.
That would do.
Both Anna&rsquo;s door and the one across the hall stood open.
Sonia stopped on the landing and listened.
No sign of struggle. No raised voice. No broken glass. From across the hall came a man&rsquo;s voice, low and indistinct, then Anna&rsquo;s lighter one answering in the softened formal register that had already started to feel like an affront.
Sonia stepped toward Anna&rsquo;s doorway first. The apartment looked orderly, almost aggressively so. No note. No bag left open in haste. Whatever had happened, Anna had not been dragged anywhere.
Heels sounded behind her, close and quick despite the shortened step.
&ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo;
Relief hit first. Then the rest of it.
Anna came out of the neighbor&rsquo;s apartment carrying a folded dish towel she seemed not to realize she still held. She wore a cream blouse closed high at the throat, a sand-colored skirt that held her legs to a disciplined line, satin gloves, and a matching hijab. A wide straw hat hung from one hand. She looked polished, composed, and completely at ease inside a version of femininity that should have read as costume and somehow no longer did.
Sonia saw the problem at once. It was deeper than the clothes.
Anna believed them.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m sorry about yesterday,&rdquo; Sonia said before Anna could speak. &ldquo;I pushed too hard and made it worse. I need your help, and I am trying not to make your life harder while I ask for it.&rdquo;
She lifted one gloved hand, a bitter little gesture toward her own outfit.
&ldquo;I got the memo.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s mouth softened.
&ldquo;Thank you for saying so.&rdquo;
Then, with a glance toward the open door behind her:
&ldquo;Will you come in for a moment? I should introduce you properly.&rdquo;
Should, Sonia thought. Not want. Should.
Sonia almost refused on reflex. Then she saw the shape of the room beyond Anna&rsquo;s shoulder and understood that this mattered to Anna in a way refusal would only complicate.
&ldquo;All right.&rdquo;
She followed Anna into Julien Moreau&rsquo;s apartment.
It was clean enough to look recently cleaned. Deep cleaned. Kitchen counters clear. Groceries put away. Throw blanket folded. Medicine packaging collected into a neat stack by the bin. Evidence everywhere of feminine labor quietly completed and made invisible.
Julien stood near the table in shirtsleeves, one hand braced on the chair back. He still looked like someone climbing out of a fever: pale, not fully steady, annoyed at the fact of his own weakness. He was also watching Anna in a way Sonia did not like at all, with the focused attention of a man who had learned overnight that approval from him mattered.
Anna curtsied.
&ldquo;Sir, this is my friend, the late Volker Becker&rsquo;s Sonia.&rdquo;
The words landed hard. Sonia had not heard Anna present herself through a man before. Father first. Now dead fiance for Sonia. The old order had been retrofitted into their introductions as if it had always been there.
Anna turned slightly.
&ldquo;Sonia, this is Julien Moreau.&rdquo;
Julien nodded to Sonia, neither impolite nor warm, only careful.
&ldquo;Miss Sonia.&rdquo;
Sonia gave him a shallow approximation of Anna&rsquo;s curtsy because standing there like an unchanged challenge would have been stupid.
&ldquo;Sir.&rdquo;
The word tasted ridiculous.
Julien&rsquo;s eyes flicked over her outfit, registered the compromise of the beret, and moved on. He was new enough to all this that he did not seem to know whether to correct her or let it pass.
Interesting.
Anna set the dish towel down and folded her hands.
&ldquo;Sir, if it is agreeable to you, Sonia and I should leave for work.&rdquo;
Should leave. Agreeable to you.
Sonia felt the sentence like a slap and kept her face still.
Julien looked from Anna to Sonia and back again. His uncertainty showed, but so did the new instinct under it, the one that expected to be asked.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Go on. And Anna, take care on the stairs.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
Something changed in her on those two words, not outwardly by much, but in a tiny drop of the shoulders and a softening around the mouth, satisfaction so quick and involuntary that Sonia might have missed it if she had not been staring for exactly that kind of thing.
Julien saw it too.
His attention sharpened.
&ldquo;Good girl,&rdquo; he said, quietly, as if testing the phrase and finding it answered.
Anna closed her eyes for a beat.
When she opened them again, her face was composed, but color had risen under her skin. She looked steadier after the praise, not less. Settled.
So that was real: not metaphor or guesswork, but reward.
Sonia filed it away and hated the part of herself that was impressed by the elegance of the mechanism.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir,&rdquo; Anna said.
Julien&rsquo;s gaze lingered on her another second.
&ldquo;Go along, then.&rdquo;
Outside in the corridor, Anna replaced the hat over her covered hair and adjusted it with practiced fingers.
Sonia waited until they were halfway down the first flight before speaking.
&ldquo;You were in there cleaning for him.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And that was normal?&rdquo;
Anna glanced back over her shoulder.
&ldquo;It was useful.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That wasn&rsquo;t what I asked.&rdquo;
Anna made the stairs in the small sideways rhythm her skirt required, one hand on the rail, the other gathering the fabric clear. Sonia had to mimic the same constrained descent and hated that she had already learned enough to do it.
&ldquo;It is normal now,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;And before you ask, yes, I know what you mean by that question.&rdquo;
Sonia almost lost a step.
&ldquo;Do you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes. I know I was Changed.&rdquo;
She said it evenly, as if discussing weather or train times.
They reached the street and turned toward GAIA. Morning Geneva moved around them in skirts, coats, gloves, dark shoes, the occasional flash of bright lipstick, the quieter authority of suited men threading through it all. Sonia heard heels on pavement everywhere now. Felt her own answering them.
&ldquo;Then help me understand this,&rdquo; Sonia said. &ldquo;You know something was done to you, and you still talk as if this is right.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression did not crack.
&ldquo;Because knowing it was done to me does not undo that it fits.&rdquo;
There it was again. No confusion. No plea to be rescued.
Sonia tried another angle.
&ldquo;Yesterday you still sounded divided.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I was tired yesterday.&rdquo;
Anna adjusted one glove at the wrist.
&ldquo;And you were shouting at me in public.&rdquo;
Fair enough.
They crossed at the light. A tram hissed past, full of women in gloves and skirts. All looked quiet and content in their style of dress.
Sonia lowered her voice.
&ldquo;What about Julien?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s answer came too quickly to be invented.
&ldquo;He is kind. He is adjusting well.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s not what I mean either.&rdquo;
That brought the faintest pause.
&ldquo;His approval has an effect on me.&rdquo;
Direct. Calm. Infuriating.
&ldquo;An effect,&rdquo; Sonia repeated.
Anna turned her head and met her eyes fully for the first time since they had left the apartment.
&ldquo;A reward response, if you prefer technical language.&rdquo;
Sonia stared.
Anna went on in the same maddeningly level tone.
&ldquo;It is stronger from him than when I correct myself alone. The difference is quite obvious.&rdquo;
She knew. She knew exactly what was happening. And the knowledge was doing nothing to loosen it.
If anything, it had been incorporated. Named. Filed. Accepted.
Sonia felt the real shape of the problem then. Anna was not trapped by ignorance. She was stabilized by lucidity.
&ldquo;That should horrify you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;At moments it does.&rdquo;
Anna said it without drama.
&ldquo;Then he says something kind, or gives me a clear instruction, or looks pleased with me, and my body answers before the rest of me has finished objecting. It becomes difficult to care about the objection for very long.&rdquo;
The honesty of that unsettled Sonia more than denial would have.
&ldquo;You hear yourself, right?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Perfectly.&rdquo;
They walked in silence for a few steps. Sonia felt the gloves at her wrists, the reduced stride enforced by the underlayer, the social relief of passing without comment in borrowed propriety. Tactical camouflage. Useful.
Also working.
That irritated her enough to clear her thinking.
&ldquo;I dreamed the same things you did,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Or close enough. Proper behavior. Dresses. Chinese. Makeup. The whole package. But I can ignore it.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes moved over Sonia&rsquo;s outfit with quiet precision.
&ldquo;Can you?&rdquo;
Sonia almost snapped back. Instead she looked ahead and let the question sit.
No, not entirely. She had not put this outfit on for pleasure, but she had still put it on. She had read the street correctly. She had adjusted. She had asked Anna for help instead of trying to tear her back into an older shape by force.
That was not surrender, but it was not nothing either.
&ldquo;I can choose against it,&rdquo; Sonia said.
&ldquo;That is useful.&rdquo;
The answer came with no envy in it. If anything, Anna sounded faintly sorry for her.
&ldquo;For what it is worth,&rdquo; Anna added, &ldquo;I do not think this is temporary.&rdquo;
Sonia turned to her sharply.
&ldquo;What do you mean?&rdquo;
Anna kept walking, neat and composed beside her.
&ldquo;I mean the contradictions are stable. I know I was changed. I know parts of my history were rewritten. I also know I prefer what I am now. Those things do not cancel each other out. They support each other.&rdquo;
The plainness of it chilled Sonia more than any fever dream had.
&ldquo;Because once I understand what has happened,&rdquo; Anna said, &ldquo;I can arrange myself around it properly instead of fighting shadows.&rdquo;
They were close enough to GAIA now to see the glass and steel of the campus through the trees.
&ldquo;That makes it harder to reverse,&rdquo; Sonia said.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
No hesitation. No false comfort.
Then Anna looked at her, and for a moment the old intelligence was visible with painful clarity inside the new arrangement.
&ldquo;But you should still try,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;You are different from me, and that difference may matter. Just do not mistake my clarity for hidden resistance. I am not waiting for you to restore me to an older version I no longer want.&rdquo;
That landed harder than anything else she had said all morning.
Sonia believed her.
That was the real loss: not that Anna had forgotten herself, but that she remembered and approved.
They reached the entrance together, two women in proper dress moving easily enough through a changed city for no one to look twice.
Sonia felt absurd, constrained, furious, and newly certain of one thing: if there was a way to break the virus, she would have to find it without counting on Anna to want saving.
Sonia and Anna are walking to GAIA. To appease Anna&rsquo;s sensibilities and to blend in, Sonia dresses &ldquo;properly&rdquo;.

Anna and Julien# While Sonia was in Shanghai, Anna&rsquo;s life narrowed and deepened around Julien.
It did not happen all at once. That was part of why it felt so natural. One morning became several. Breakfast became a habit. Being expected became a pleasure so steady she started waking a few minutes before the alarm, already warm with it.
One morning, she dressed with more care than usual because Julien had told her to come early.
Stockings first, clipped to a girdle. Then heels, enough height to alter her carriage without making the stairs impossible. The Yoyah Sheengzoh came next, drawn up and settled into place. A nude longline bra that sculpted her figure, the stiff collar of her blouse reminding her not to slouch and not to forget herself. She finished with a dark blue skirt, a cream hijab, gloves, soft makeup, well aware that she was making herself pleasant for him to look at.
That thought no longer embarrassed her; she was comfortable in that knowledge.
She let herself into his apartment with the key he had given her and paused just inside the door. Quiet. Early light. The apartment still held the trace of sleep and the faint smell of yesterday&rsquo;s coffee.
She went to his bedroom and stopped a respectful step from the bed.
&ldquo;Sir,&rdquo; she said softly. &ldquo;Good morning.&rdquo;
Julien woke without jolting. He had started doing that. The new order sat in him more comfortably now, but not carelessly. He looked at her, took in the outfit, the folded hands, the fact that she had come as asked, and his face changed in that way she had already learned to crave.
Approval first. Then warmth.
&ldquo;Good morning, Anna,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Come here.&rdquo;
She stepped closer.
&ldquo;Thank you for being prompt.&rdquo;
The words settled low in her body, less sharply than good girl did, but with the same direction.
&ldquo;You are welcome, Sir.&rdquo;
His gaze moved over her once more, not greedily, simply with attention.
&ldquo;You look very pretty.&rdquo;
That reached her even deeper. She lowered her eyes to contain the reaction and felt herself smile despite the effort.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
Julien sat up and rubbed one hand over his face.
&ldquo;I am still getting used to this,&rdquo; he said, half to himself. &ldquo;Having someone here. Expecting someone.&rdquo;
Anna lifted her head just enough to answer.
&ldquo;I am glad to be expected, Sir.&rdquo;
He looked at her for a second longer than the sentence required.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;I think you are.&rdquo;
That, too, felt good.
&ldquo;Go on,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Make breakfast. Then come back.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
In the kitchen she moved quickly and carefully, already knowing where he kept the pans, the eggs, the butter, the better coffee. She liked the practical intimacy of that knowledge. It made the space feel partially hers, though only through service.
She set the table, cooked, poured, arranged, then returned to wait for him in the posture her mother had once taught and the virus had restored to absolute importance: still, hands folded, shoulders open, gaze lowered, ready.
When Julien came out dressed for work, he stopped and looked at the table, then at her.
&ldquo;Very nice,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Serve.&rdquo;
She did.
The first days taught them both that he liked being looked after and that she liked being directed through the small domestic details that had once seemed beneath notice. Coffee poured for him at the right strength. Eggs set down hot. His bag placed by the door before he remembered to ask. Her body answered each successful anticipation with relief and readiness. The work was ordinary. The approval was not.
One morning, after tasting the eggs, Julien glanced at the second plate and then at her waiting posture.
&ldquo;Are you going to stand there until I tell you to eat?&rdquo;
Anna hesitated.
&ldquo;If that is what you prefer, Sir.&rdquo;
Something softened in his expression, closer to care made firmer by authority than to pity.
&ldquo;Bring the cushion,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Kneel here.&rdquo;
Her pulse jumped.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
She fetched it from where she had quietly begun leaving it near the dining table and placed it by his chair. Kneeling there felt at once humiliating and perfect. Her skirt settled around her legs, the underlayer and heels keeping her in a carefully limited posture even now. She folded her hands and waited.
Julien cut a piece of toast, added avocado, and held it toward her.
&ldquo;Open.&rdquo;
She did.
The bite was small, simple, and almost impossibly intimate. She took it from his fingers, chewed, swallowed, and felt heat rise all through her.
Julien watched her closely.
&ldquo;Well?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Very good, Sir.&rdquo;
He fed her again.
It became their habit after that, not every morning, but often enough that Anna started hoping for it the moment she laid the table. Julien was attentive in a way that made obedience easier to bear and therefore more dangerous. He remembered what she liked. He noticed if the coffee was too bitter for her, if the toast scratched, if she had a faint mark at her waist from yesterday&rsquo;s belt. He would tell her to kneel, then feed her carefully, then praise her for the meal she had made with her own hands.
The contradiction should have felt absurd.
Instead it made sense in the place inside her that had been taught to answer care with surrender.
At dinner he was less hurried, more settled. They fell into a second ritual there. Anna cooked. Julien tasted the first bite. If pleased, he said so. Sometimes he told her to sit. Sometimes to kneel. Once, after she served roast chicken with potatoes and thyme, he said &ldquo;Good Girl&rdquo; so softly it felt almost private, and the force of it made her grip the edge of the table to stay steady.
He noticed and put down his fork.
&ldquo;Come here.&rdquo;
She crossed the room and stopped beside him.
&ldquo;Look at me.&rdquo;
She did. It took effort now. The reward had a way of making her want to hide inside the posture it created.
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t say this to toy with you,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Do you understand that?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good. I say it because you do well, and because I like what it does to you.&rdquo;
There was nothing cruel in it. Anna breathed in carefully.
&ldquo;I like what it does to me too, Sir.&rdquo;
Julien&rsquo;s mouth curved at one corner.
&ldquo;I had gathered that.&rdquo;
Another evening, when the dishes were done and the apartment had fallen quiet around them, Anna knelt beside his chair with her hands on her lap and worked up the courage to ask for something she had been turning over in her mind for days.
&ldquo;Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes?&rdquo;
&ldquo;May I ask a question?&rdquo;
&ldquo;You may.&rdquo;
She chose the words with care.
&ldquo;Would it please you if I dressed more&hellip; seriously?&rdquo;
Julien looked down at her.
&ldquo;More seriously how?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Corsets,&rdquo; Anna said, then forced herself not to rush. &ldquo;And some of the new blouses at Maylee Shufoo. The more structured ones. I have been thinking about them.&rdquo;
He was quiet for long enough to make her pulse climb.
&ldquo;Have you,&rdquo; he said at last.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;For yourself?&rdquo;
The question landed directly.
&ldquo;For both of us, Sir.&rdquo;
Julien leaned back, considering her with an attention that made her feel as if he were adjusting something in her without touching her.
&ldquo;Tell me why.&rdquo;
Anna swallowed.
&ldquo;Because I think I would wear them well. Because I want to please you. Because I want&hellip;&rdquo; She paused, hating the heat in her face and unable to stop it. &ldquo;I want to be more fully what I am becoming.&rdquo;
Julien did not rescue her from the sentence. He made her stay in it.
&ldquo;And what is that?&rdquo;
Her voice came out softer.
&ldquo;Yours, if that is acceptable to you, Sir.&rdquo;
The silence after that was not long, but it changed the room.
Julien set his glass down.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Go and have a proper fitting. Choose carefully. Then show me.&rdquo;
The relief was so immediate she nearly swayed with it.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And Anna.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Do not choose things that only look dramatic. Choose things that truly suit you.&rdquo;
She felt absurdly cherished by the practicality of that.
&ldquo;I will, Sir.&rdquo;
Lily recognized her the moment she entered Maylee Shufoo.
&ldquo;Anna. Back already.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
Anna smiled despite herself.
&ldquo;I am looking at corsets now.&rdquo;
Lily glanced once over Anna&rsquo;s current silhouette and nodded as if the decision had merely arrived on schedule.
&ldquo;That follows.&rdquo;
She did not ask intrusive questions. That was one of the things Anna liked about her. Lily understood the pleasure of being arranged without pretending it was innocent or reducing it to a speech.
&ldquo;Daywear, evening, or sleep?&rdquo; Lily asked as she led Anna inward.
&ldquo;Daywear first.&rdquo;
&ldquo;For support or for effect?&rdquo;
Anna thought of Julien&rsquo;s eyes on her, of the way praise had started reaching further into her body than thought could follow.
&ldquo;Both.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sensibly answered.&rdquo;
Lily scanned her measurements, checked fit, and drew three options. One was soft enough for gradual wear. One was firmer and elegant. The last had shoulder shaping built into it and promised a more dramatic line than anything Anna had worn before.
&ldquo;Try this one second,&rdquo; Lily said, setting the firmest aside for later. &ldquo;You need to know the difference.&rdquo;
The first corset was serious but tolerable. It narrowed her, straightened her, made every breath more considered. Anna liked it at once.
That worried her.
Lily saw the look and gave a small nod.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;That is usually how it starts.&rdquo;
The second corset was the one that mattered.
Lily laced her in slowly, pausing whenever Anna needed to adjust, then tightened again. The pressure gathered around her waist and ribs with deliberate patience. Her shoulders drew back. Her lower spine curved. Her chest rose. By the time Lily tied the last knot, Anna could feel the whole architecture of the garment dictating how she would stand, turn, sit, bend, and ask for help.
She opened her eyes to the mirror and almost did not know herself.
The line was beautiful. So was the loss of ease.
&ldquo;Breathe,&rdquo; Lily said.
&ldquo;I am.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You are thinking about breathing. That is different.&rdquo;
Anna let out a small breath that made them both smile.
&ldquo;It is tight.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It should be. Correctly, not cruelly.&rdquo;
Lily moved around behind her and adjusted the shoulder structure.
&ldquo;There. Better. This one wants a certain woman. If you fight it, it will exhaust you. If you cooperate, it becomes very persuasive.&rdquo;
That was true at once. Fighting the line of it made her feel trapped. Yielding to it made her feel arranged, finished, held.
Anna touched the front busk lightly.
&ldquo;Julien will like this.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I expect so.&rdquo;
Lily brought her one of the newer blouses afterward, a Gowbao Yoohoo in pale silk with a high reinforced collar and cuffs that were much too elaborate for independent dressing. The fabric was lovely. The cut was not kind.
&ldquo;Arms,&rdquo; Lily said.
Anna obeyed.
The blouse went on over the corset in stages: sleeves guided into place, cuffs closed one knot after another, the back fastened for her, the collar secured last. By the time Lily finished, Anna&rsquo;s body belonged to a very narrow set of acceptable movements. She could stand beautifully. She could turn with care. She could not slump, tug, reach back, or free herself.
Her chin lifted slightly under the collar&rsquo;s guidance. Her gaze fell without needing to be told.
The result in the mirror was almost shocking.
Elegant. Helpless. Radiant.
Anna felt a pulse of shame at how much she liked that third part because of the second.
&ldquo;Too much?&rdquo; Lily asked.
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; Anna said, then corrected herself into honesty. &ldquo;Yes. But in a useful way.&rdquo;
Lily&rsquo;s expression softened into something almost approving.
&ldquo;Exactly.&rdquo;
Anna turned as far as the blouse allowed and felt at once how much of the rest would require assistance. Dressing. Undressing. Bathroom logistics. Reaching dropped things. Endurance. Planning.
Julien&rsquo;s hands at her back in the morning. Julien&rsquo;s hands at her throat in the evening, undoing the collar.
Her breath changed.
Lily noticed that too and had the decency not to comment.
&ldquo;Take a few steps,&rdquo; she said.
Anna did. The corset held her, the blouse corrected her, the collar guided her head, and her underskirt finished the work below. Every part of her outfit now collaborated with every other part. Grace was no longer a preference; it was the only option.
She stopped before the mirror again.
&ldquo;I cannot manage this alone.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; Lily said. &ldquo;That is part of why it works.&rdquo;
Anna looked at herself for one more long moment.
The woman in the mirror would need help to dress. Help to undress. Help to endure long hours gracefully. She would receive that help from a man whose approval already went through her like heat.
The thought was so intimate it almost counted as a confession.
&ldquo;I will take this one,&rdquo; she said.
Lily helped her out of the set slowly, which was an education of its own. Relief came in stages. So did disappointment.
By the time Anna carried the boxes home, she was flushed, sore, and impatient for evening in a way that made her feel younger and more womanly at once.
Julien opened the door before she could use her key.
His eyes went to the boxes, then to her face.
&ldquo;That promising?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He stepped aside to let her in.
&ldquo;Show me after dinner.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s whole body answered before her mouth could.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
Dinner that night was almost impossible to get through with dignity. Julien noticed. Of course he noticed.
&ldquo;You are distracted.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Excited?&rdquo;
There was no safe answer but the true one.
&ldquo;Very, Sir.&rdquo;
His expression sharpened with pleasure.
&ldquo;So am I. I am looking forward to what you have to show me.&rdquo;
Later, in the bedroom, he stood behind her while she opened the first box with careful fingers.
&ldquo;Tell me what you chose,&rdquo; he said.
Anna lifted the corset out by its straps and turned enough for him to see it.
&ldquo;A day corset, Sir. And a blouse to wear over it. The blouse cannot be managed alone.&rdquo;
Julien took the blouse from her hands, and inspected it with quiet interest.
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t think it can.&rdquo;
Anna looked at him through the mirror.
&ldquo;Would you help me, Sir?&rdquo;
He met her eyes there.
&ldquo;Yes. Undress.&rdquo;
The word went through her like a command and a caress at once.
She obeyed, slower than usual because she wanted him to see what she was offering, and because the knowledge that he would soon be the one fastening her into something she could not escape had already begun to color every breath.
By the time she stood in her stockings and bra, Julien had gone very still.
He did not touch her at once. He looked first, openly and with concentration, as if the thing she was offering him deserved to be studied before it was handled. That alone made heat move low and slow through her.
&ldquo;Come here,&rdquo; he said.
She crossed to him on careful feet.
Julien set the S-line corset around her waist and drew her hair forward over one shoulder.
&ldquo;Hold still.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
The busk closed first. Then the laces. Julien tightened them in stages, not roughly, but with the steady patience of a man adjusting something that mattered. Each pull changed her a little more. Her waist drew in. Her spine arched. Her shoulders opened. When he fastened the shoulder straps and settled them properly, her posture shifted again, less fit for work now, more obviously meant to be seen.
Anna watched it happen in the mirror and felt her breathing change with each correction.
&ldquo;Too tight?&rdquo; he asked.
&ldquo;No, Sir.&rdquo;
He tested that answer with one more deliberate pull.
Her breath broke around a small sound she could not hide.
Julien&rsquo;s eyes lifted to hers in the glass.
&ldquo;Still all right?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
His mouth moved slightly, not quite a smile.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
That one word alone was enough to leave her weak in the knees.
The underskirt came next. Julien gathered the dress up enough for her to step into it, then fastened it under the corseted waist himself. The moment it settled into place, she felt the familiar narrowing at her thighs and knees, the limit laid on every future step she would take in it.
&ldquo;Again,&rdquo; he said softly. &ldquo;Step.&rdquo;
She tried one careful pace and felt the constraint answer at once.
&ldquo;Very good,&rdquo; he said.
The dress was worse in the best possible way.
Julien buttoned Anna&rsquo;s dress slowly from behind.
It was darker than the blouse she had tried at the shop, more severe, cut to work with the corset rather than soften it. Julien held it open for her and guided her arms into place, then drew it up over the corset with a patience that made the whole thing feel ceremonial. Once the fabric settled, he moved behind her and began fastening the long back closure.
Button by button.
Then the sleeves, each row closed at her wrists by fingers that were not hers.
The high collar came last.
When he secured it, her chin lifted a little and her gaze fell without permission. Her body accepted the new limits at once. She could feel how little freedom remained in her neck, shoulders, and arms. She would not reach easily in this. She would not undress herself. She would not do much of anything carelessly.
Julien knelt to fasten her heels.
That almost undid her.
The whole sequence had been too intimate already, but this made the helplessness plain. She stood there while he settled the straps around her ankles, the dress and corset holding her upright for inspection, for display, for care.
When he rose, Anna barely recognized the woman in the mirror.
The S-line corset pushed her forward into a posture that was equal parts invitation and restraint. The shoulder straps held her open. The collar made her look composed even while it denied her ordinary movement. The back-buttoned dress closed her in. The underskirt made any natural stride impossible. The heels tipped her weight just enough to turn stillness into a pose.
This was not for work.
This was to be looked at in.
And helped in.
Her face grew warm.
&ldquo;Turn for me,&rdquo; Julien said.
She obeyed as best she could, discovering the dress in the act of moving through it. It took several small, managed steps to do what would once have been one easy pivot. By the time she faced him again, his gaze was fixed on her with a kind of quiet hunger that made her pulse jump.
&ldquo;Beautiful,&rdquo; he said.
The praise went through her so hard she had to steady herself on the dressing table.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
He stepped closer and touched the side of her waist, then the line of the sleeve, then the fall of the skirt over her hips, small proprietary adjustments that made it impossible to pretend she had dressed for herself alone.
&ldquo;Can you manage alone in that?&rdquo; he asked.
Anna looked at herself in the mirror and answered honestly.
&ldquo;Not well, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Tell me.&rdquo;
The directness of it made her swallow.
&ldquo;I can stand. Walk slowly. Sit with care, if I am helped. I cannot dress myself. I cannot undress myself. I will need help with the buttons, the collar, and anything I drop. Quite a lot, really.&rdquo;
Julien absorbed that with visible satisfaction, but not cruelty.
&ldquo;Then you will ask when you need help.&rdquo;
That simple permission made pleasure tighten through her again.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He kept his hand at her waist, warm and steady.
&ldquo;This is not a practical dress.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;This is something to be displayed in.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
His thumb moved once against the fabric.
&ldquo;And looked after in.&rdquo;
The sentence struck even deeper than the praise had. Her arousal sharpened at once, threaded through with relief so intense it nearly felt like gratitude. That was the truth of it. If she had been left alone in this, it would have become ordeal. With him there, fastening her into it and promising himself as the answer to everything it took away, it became something else entirely.
Something warmer.
Something frighteningly sweet.
&ldquo;I like that, Sir,&rdquo; she said.
Julien watched her through the mirror.
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Walk to me.&rdquo;
She obeyed, crossing the room in the tiny managed steps the dress demanded. By the time she reached him, the helplessness of the outfit had become a physical fact she could not ignore. He was the one who had put her into it. He would be the one to let her out again. In between, her body would have to move through the world on the terms he had helped impose.
And both of them liked that.
Julien took one of her gloved hands and raised it to his mouth.
&ldquo;Good girl,&rdquo; he said softly.
The reward shivered through her from throat to thighs. Her breath caught. Her knees weakened. If he had not still been holding her hand and watching her with that calm, caretaking attention, the dress might have frightened her.
Instead it made her ache.
&ldquo;You feel it,&rdquo; he said.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Say it.&rdquo;
Her face burned, but she obeyed.
&ldquo;I need help in this.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
His thumb moved once over her knuckles.
&ldquo;And you like needing it from me.&rdquo;
There was no room left for evasion.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
Julien drew her a little closer.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
That word carried praise, possession, permission, and care in proportions she could no longer separate. Anna stood before him in the impossible dress, unable to free herself from it, unable to move naturally in it, and so aroused by the combination of display and dependence that she could barely think straight.
The worst part was how safe it felt.
Or perhaps that was the best part.

A Giving-Away Ceremony# Anna did not witness the private conversation between Julien and her father.
That was as it should be.
She waited in Marc Lefevre&rsquo;s sitting room with her hands folded in her lap and her back too straight to be comfortable, listening to the low murmur of male voices through the closed door and feeling each passing minute as a small tightening in her chest.
She already knew what she wanted. That was not the same as knowing what would be decided.
When the door opened, Julien stepped out first. Marc followed a moment later, composed as ever, though his eyes were softer than usual when they settled on her.
Anna rose at once.
&ldquo;Sir?&rdquo;
Marc gave a small nod.
&ldquo;It is agreed.&rdquo;
The words went through her so quickly she had to catch herself on posture alone. Relief came first. Then gratitude. Then the deeper, stranger sensation underneath both: that the shape of her future had just been made formal by two men discussing her over a closed door and that this, rather than humiliating her, had filled her with peace.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir,&rdquo; she said.
She meant both of them.
Julien&rsquo;s hand touched the middle of her back for only a second.
&ldquo;Steady,&rdquo; he murmured.
It helped.
Later that week she and Julien went to the Jade Garden to settle the details.
Anna chose it almost at once. The tea house sat north of Geneva, a western take on a formal Asian tea house, designed for functions just like hers. Gravel paths, dark wood, trimmed pines, water moving quietly over stone. It was beautiful without being showy. Ceremonial without becoming theatrical. A place where a woman could be transferred from one man&rsquo;s care to another in perfect calm.
Julien watched her take it in.
&ldquo;This one?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He looked over the pond, the curved bridge, the carved screens of the main room.
&ldquo;It suits you.&rdquo;
That pleased her absurdly.
They walked the path slowly because Anna&rsquo;s clothes required it and because neither of them seemed in a hurry to leave. Julien asked practical questions. Guest count. Timing. Privacy. The room for tea afterward. Anna answered where she could and deferred where she should. Once or twice he settled a decision with a simple &ldquo;we will do it that way,&rdquo; and each time she felt the same deep easing she had come to associate with being competently directed.
The guest list remained small.
Marc, of course. A few people from Julien&rsquo;s side. A few from hers. No one noisy. No one careless. The day did not want a crowd.
&ldquo;Sonia,&rdquo; Anna said, before she could overthink it.
Julien glanced at her.
&ldquo;If she is back in time?&rdquo;
&ldquo;If she is back in time, Sir.&rdquo;
He considered that without jealousy or irritation, which made Anna love him more.
&ldquo;If you want her there, invite her.&rdquo;
Anna lowered her eyes.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
That part mattered more than she wanted to admit. Sonia had not approved of any of this. Sonia might never approve of any of it. But Anna wanted her there anyway, wanted her to see that this was not confusion anymore, not fever damage, not a phase that would pass if handled correctly. This was her life now. Chosen, even within the shape of what had been done to her. Especially there.
The dress took longer.
Lily received the news with exactly the right degree of pleasure, enough to feel warm, not enough to become vulgar.
&ldquo;So,&rdquo; she said, drawing out the word as she circled Anna with measuring eyes. &ldquo;Now you need to be given away properly.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s face warmed.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
Lily pulled fabrics, sketched lines, laid options against Anna&rsquo;s skin and stepped back to study the effect. The foundation, they all agreed, would remain strict. Corset. Controlled movement. Proper posture. The visible layers could be made softer, more ceremonial, but what mattered would still be there underneath: the architecture that held her where she belonged.
&ldquo;Nothing too bridal in the old sense,&rdquo; Lily said. &ldquo;This is not innocence.&rdquo;
No. It was not.
&ldquo;And nothing too severe either,&rdquo; Lily went on. &ldquo;He is not acquiring furniture.&rdquo;
Anna could not help smiling.
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
What they arrived at was more elegant than girlish: a formal silhouette built over restraint, with Chinese details worked into the surface so the whole thing felt less like costume than like an evolved local ritual. Beautiful, but not free. Refined, but unmistakably dependent. Lily spoke of drape, line, and occasion. Anna heard all of that and also the quieter truth beneath it: Julien would be able to look at her and see, before anyone said a word, that she had come ready to be handed into his care.
That thought stayed with her all evening.
So did the language.
Giving away. Into his care. Julien Moreau&rsquo;s Anna.
None of it frightened her anymore, not really. It made her restless, tender, and a little ashamed of how much she liked imagining the formal surrender of it: her father&rsquo;s hand, Julien&rsquo;s acceptance, the visible shift in how others would address her afterward, the tea she would pour, the first quiet private minutes after, when the ritual would be over and the reality would still remain.
That night, back in Julien&rsquo;s apartment, she knelt beside his chair while he reviewed a seating note from the Jade Garden and made one or two final practical corrections. The domesticity of that nearly undid her more than the grander thoughts had.
&ldquo;You are distracted again,&rdquo; he said without looking up.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He set the paper aside then and gave her his full attention.
&ldquo;Anxious?&rdquo;
Anna thought about it.
&ldquo;Expectant, Sir.&rdquo;
That made him smile.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
The word moved through her with familiar force.
&ldquo;It is still some weeks away,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;You do not need to live in the future before it arrives.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;But you may look forward to it.&rdquo;
The permission felt almost as intimate as touch.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He reached out and touched her jaw lightly, two fingers under the line of it, not enough to move her, only enough to remind her that he could.
&ldquo;I am looking forward to it too.&rdquo;
Anna lowered her gaze because there was too much in her face to show safely.
That was the state she remained in after that: not completed, not yet formally transferred, but no longer in doubt. Waiting had become part of the pleasure. The ceremony was still ahead of her. Sonia was still away. Her life had settled into a new shape, and the last thing left was to stand still long enough to be given over publicly into the future she already inhabited in private.

A Maid&rsquo;s Work# Sonia&rsquo;s placement into Cheng&rsquo;s household happened faster than it should have.
That was what frightened her.
The interview lasted less than twenty minutes. Mrs. Huang looked over her papers, asked practical questions about tray service, laundering delicate fabrics, flower care, discretion, and family background, then watched her kneel to present tea without spilling a drop. Sonia kept her eyes lowered, her answers short, her accent softened where Mei Lin had coached it. The corset held her shoulders back. The narrow line of the skirt kept her steps modest. Even her fear helped. It made her careful.
Mrs. Huang took the cup from the tray, tasted it, and set it down.
&ldquo;Turn.&rdquo;
Sonia obeyed.
&ldquo;Again.&rdquo;
She turned back.
Mrs. Huang studied the fit of the dress, the line of Sonia&rsquo;s posture, the composure she kept wrapped around her own body like another layer of clothing.
&ldquo;You have worked in formal households before?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And you understand that staff here are not seen unless needed?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang&rsquo;s gaze did not warm, but it settled.
&ldquo;Good. We prefer women who understand the value of invisibility.&rdquo;
By evening Sonia had a room under the roof and a gray dress hanging from a hook beside the bed.
The room was narrower than the one she had used in training and cleaner in a way that felt less kind. Bed. Basin. Wardrobe. Small shelf. One high window that gave her a slice of sky and almost nothing else. The villa kept its beauty for the rooms that mattered. Up here the point was not beauty. It was order.
She put her bag on the bed and stood in the middle of the room for a moment with her hands hanging at her sides.
This was success. This was the closest she had come to Cheng, to proof, to the source of what had been done to the world.
It was also an attic maid&rsquo;s room in a house built by a man who might have engineered the end of the life she had understood as normal.
She unpacked quickly because there was nothing worth arranging. A few changes of underthings. Toiletries. Phone. Chargers. The documents that proved she was not really who the household records said she was stayed hidden where they needed to stay hidden. When she finished, the room looked exactly as impersonal as before.
In the morning the house dressed her properly.
The uniform was stricter than what Mei Lin had used in training and more anonymous. Gray from throat to hem. High collar. Long sleeves. White apron. Gloves for service. The corset underneath came with shoulder braces that discouraged softness in the spine. Beneath the dress, a narrow underskirt shortened her stride and kept her knees honest. There was no decorative charm to any of it. This was not the flirtation of boutique restraint. This was livery. A woman made orderly, serviceable, and interchangeable.
The maid in the mirror had no edges anyone needed to remember.
At roll call the women stood in a line outside the service pantry while Mrs. Huang inspected them one by one. Powder even. Hair controlled. Shoes clean. Aprons straight. Sonia held herself still and let her face go blank. Around her the other maids smelled faintly of starch, soap, and the warm press of bodies already laced and fastened for the day.
Mrs. Huang stopped in front of a younger maid and adjusted the girl&rsquo;s cuff with two sharp fingers.
&ldquo;Crooked,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Do it again and begin the morning from the start.&rdquo;
The girl bowed her head at once.
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang moved on.
Nothing in her voice rose. Nothing needed to. The household did not run on shouting. It ran on certainty.
That was worse.
Sonia learned the rhythm fast because she had been trained for it and because the house left no room for failure to become personal. A maid either worked or waited to work. There was no third state. She laid breakfast trays, polished silver, dusted carved screens, carried pressed linen, refreshed flowers, and stood by doors with her hands placed correctly while conversations happened past her as if she were a useful furnishing.
The anonymity had weight.
In Mei Lin&rsquo;s flat Sonia had still felt watched as Sonia. Here she disappeared into function. The women beside her moved with the same small steps, the same lowered gaze, the same quick economy at the wrists. The house swallowed individuality and returned efficiency.
It would have been easier to resist if it had felt uglier.
Instead it felt smooth.
One afternoon Mr. Chen stopped beside her as she finished arranging tea things in a downstairs sitting room.
&ldquo;Miss Sonia.&rdquo;
She turned at once and lowered her eyes.
&ldquo;Sir?&rdquo;
He inspected the tray without touching it. Mr. Chen was a thin man in late middle age, always precise, always immaculate, his authority so polished it almost disappeared into courtesy.
&ldquo;The cups are a fraction too far apart.&rdquo;
Sonia looked and saw that he was right.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m sorry, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Do not be sorry. Be exact.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He watched her reset them.
&ldquo;Better,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Dr. Cheng values harmony. Harmony is not a slogan in this house. It is visible in every object and every person who serves here.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He inclined his head and moved on.
It should have been nothing. A correction. A sentence about household standards. But the words stayed with her because they named the thing she felt everywhere around her: not chaos, not panic, not even open cruelty, but a cultivated alignment in which every woman seemed arranged for someone else&rsquo;s ease.
Mrs. Huang was less polished and more dangerous for it.
When Sonia missed a streak on the mirrored leg of a side table, the housekeeper did not raise her voice. She only stood over her and made her do the entire room again.
&ldquo;If you need praise for basic competence,&rdquo; she said, &ldquo;you are in the wrong house.&rdquo;
Sonia swallowed her resentment and curtsied.
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
The dress rustled softly as she bent. The corset pressed at her ribs. She could feel sweat collecting at the base of her spine under the braces while she worked back through the room she had already done once. By the second pass she hated Mrs. Huang. By the end of the third, when the woman finally said, &ldquo;acceptable,&rdquo; Sonia felt the ugly little pulse of relief approval always gave her now.
That was the most dangerous part.
Not the discipline.
The way her body answered it.
Days passed. The house opened itself to her in fragments: servant corridors hidden behind carved panels, dumbwaiters, polished staircases for guests and narrow service stairs for women carrying trays, the quiet wing where Cheng took private meetings, the laboratory suite accessible only to trusted staff and technical personnel. Sonia learned where not to look, which doors mattered, which guards nodded to house servants and which ones checked credentials every time.
What she learned fastest was that the house did not think of itself as cruel.
It thought of itself as correct.
Her first real look at Cheng came because Mrs. Huang sent her to fetch a briefcase from his private rooms and bring it to the conference room without delay.
&ldquo;Do not dally,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;And do not look at anything that does not concern you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
His bedroom was larger than Sonia&rsquo;s entire childhood apartment. Dark wood, restrained luxury, no clutter except the clutter of a man important enough to leave work inside his private rooms and expect others to manage the overflow discreetly. The briefcase sat half open on a side desk with papers tucked into one pocket and a tablet sleeping black in the center. Sonia took it, shut it carefully, and hurried back through the service passage, the weight awkward against her ribs.
By the time she reached the conference room she was short of breath.
She stopped just outside the hidden servant entrance, forced her shoulders down, smoothed the front of her apron, and went in.
Cheng barely glanced at her when she placed the case beside him.
He was younger than she had expected from the files. Not young, but not old either. Elegant without softness. He wore authority the way some men wore good tailoring, as if it had been measured for him and improved his posture. Around the table sat engineers, administrators, and two men in government dress. A wall screen glowed with models Sonia could not read from where she stood.
&ldquo;Leave it there,&rdquo; Cheng said.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
She retreated to the wall.
He went on speaking as if she were furniture.
&ldquo;People keep using the language of restriction,&rdquo; he said, tapping one finger lightly against the table. &ldquo;Restriction is how undisciplined cultures describe structure when structure is first imposed. Later they stop experiencing it as loss. They experience it as intelligibility.&rdquo;
One of the engineers smiled as if she had heard this before and still enjoyed it.
Cheng looked at the screen.
&ldquo;A society cannot remain stable while every appetite is treated as sovereign. Freedom without form produces noise. Anxiety. Waste. Isolation. We have corrected that.&rdquo;
A man in government dress said, &ldquo;The European response remains uneven.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Temporary,&rdquo; Cheng said. &ldquo;Women adapt first in the body, then in habit, then in memory. Men adapt first in duty. After that the institutions follow. The body teaches the lesson, and society discovers the morality that matches it.&rdquo;
Sonia stood with her hands folded and kept her face empty while nausea turned slowly under her corset.
This was what frightened her more than shouting ideologues or obvious monsters ever could have.
He sounded reasonable.
He sounded proud of making domination feel like relief.
Another engineer brought up a rendering Sonia only half saw from the side: a smooth curve over the lower face, elegant enough to look almost decorative.
Cheng&rsquo;s attention sharpened.
&ldquo;Good,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Not punitive. Aspirational. It must feel like refinement before it feels inevitable.&rdquo;
Sonia did not let herself look too long.
Mrs. Huang&rsquo;s instructions had not included standing there listening, and the room was not safe. She curtsied the smallest amount necessary and left at the next pause in conversation, Cheng already speaking again behind her about rollout, affordability, and acceptability curves as if he were discussing transit design.
That night she lay awake in her attic room listening to the house settle around her and understanding that she could not leave without something real.
Suspicion was nothing.
She needed proof.
The chance came three days later.
A senior technician spilled solvent in one of the smaller lab spaces and the floor had to be cleaned before Cheng returned from an off-site meeting. Mrs. Huang chose Sonia because she had already been trusted near the private wing and because Sonia had learned to move quickly without looking hurried.
&ldquo;Do the floor, the lower surfaces, and the sink area,&rdquo; Mrs. Huang said. &ldquo;Nothing else. If anyone enters, you stop and make yourself useful.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
The air in the lab carried the smell of alcohol and metal. White counters. Locked cabinets. Diagnostic equipment. Sonia wheeled in her cart, gloves already on, and felt her pulse begin to climb.
She cleaned what she had been told to clean first.
That mattered.
The camera in the corner would show a maid at work, not a thief lunging for secrets the second she crossed the threshold.
Only when the obvious tasks were done did she let herself drift closer to the side station where portable drives sat in a docking tray beside a terminal gone dark. Two USB sticks. One encrypted tablet. A paper folder clipped shut and marked with project initials she recognized from Mara&rsquo;s notes.
Her mouth went dry.
She kept moving.
Wipe the counter. Adjust the bottles. Lift the folder just enough to dust under it. Set it down. Slide one of the drives into the pocket hidden beneath the folded cleaning cloth. Do not rush. The air in the room felt painfully cold against the sweat gathering under her collar.
A sound in the corridor stopped her heart.
Voices. Male. Coming closer.
Sonia bent instantly over the sink and wrung out the cloth as the door opened.
Cheng entered with two researchers behind him.
He spared her one glance.
&ldquo;Almost done?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir. This maid is finishing the sink area.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
He was already talking to the others before the word left his mouth.
&ldquo;The behavioral layer is holding more cleanly in female cohorts. The reward loops are still too blunt in some male populations. I want elegance, not crudity.&rdquo;
Sonia kept her head down and cleaned the same stretch of steel twice while her heart slammed against the corset. The hidden drive felt enormous. Impossible. She could feel it even though it barely changed the weight of the cloth in her hand.
One of the researchers said, &ldquo;And the suppression protocols?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Speech limitation is socially delicate,&rdquo; Cheng replied. &ldquo;Visible coercion creates resistance. Voluntary adoption is better. Give them beauty, health logic, hygiene logic, status logic. Let silence arrive dressed as sophistication.&rdquo;
Sonia had to concentrate on her breathing to keep from showing anything on her face.
After another minute Cheng dismissed her with nothing more than, &ldquo;Out.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
She curtsied, rolled the cart away, and did not let herself walk faster until the service door had shut behind her.
In the laundry room she transferred the drive from the cloth pocket into the lining of her apron with hands that only shook a little.
Back in her room she locked the door, sat on the bed, and finally let herself breathe hard.
The drive held more than she had hoped for. Genomic annotations. Behavioral layering notes. Sex-differentiated compulsions. Memory editing references. Internal strategy language far uglier than the public rhetoric around harmony and social responsibility. Sonia copied everything to encrypted storage on her phone, uploaded a secured archive, then copied it again because fear made repetition feel like control.
When she finished, her fingers were stiff and the room felt too small to hold what she knew.
She did not sleep much.
By morning the plan had become simple.
Leave before the theft was noticed.
She went to Mrs. Huang just after breakfast service, when the housekeeper was reviewing inventory sheets and least likely to enjoy interruption.
Sonia stopped at the proper distance and folded her hands.
&ldquo;Madam, if I may request a word.&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang did not look up immediately.
&ldquo;Speak.&rdquo;
&ldquo;My mother is gravely ill. I have received word this morning. I humbly ask permission to attend her.&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang&rsquo;s head lifted.
&ldquo;Your duties are here.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Madam. I understand.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Then why are you asking?&rdquo;
Because I have stolen from the man who signs your wages and I need to be on a plane before someone checks the lab access logs.
Sonia kept her expression soft and stricken.
&ldquo;Because she is my mother, Madam.&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang&rsquo;s mouth tightened.
&ldquo;Sentiment does not keep a house running.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, Madam.&rdquo;
It might have ended there if Mr. Chen had not entered midway through the exchange.
He took in the scene with one quick glance.
&ldquo;A problem?&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang answered before Sonia could.
&ldquo;A request for leave at an inconvenient moment.&rdquo;
Mr. Chen looked at Sonia.
&ldquo;Miss Sonia?&rdquo;
She lowered her gaze further.
&ldquo;Sir, my mother is very ill. I ask permission to go to her for a short time, if the household can spare me.&rdquo;
He was quiet for long enough to feel deliberate.
&ldquo;Family obligations are not trivial,&rdquo; he said at last.
Mrs. Huang&rsquo;s face barely changed, but Sonia saw the displeasure there.
&ldquo;Nor is household continuity,&rdquo; the housekeeper replied.
&ldquo;Of course,&rdquo; Mr. Chen said mildly. &ldquo;But Dr. Cheng&rsquo;s principles do not set family against duty. They organize duty properly.&rdquo;
He turned back to Sonia.
&ldquo;You may go attend your mother. You will carry yourself as a representative of this household while you do so. Do you understand?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You will be discreet. You will remain orderly. You will return if return is possible.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
It was the most dangerous moment of the entire theft, stranger even than the lab. He was being kind to her. Not soft, not equal, but sincerely kind inside the terms of the world that had made him. That sincerity made the lie uglier.
She curtsied.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir. Thank you, Madam.&rdquo;
Mrs. Huang dismissed her with obvious reluctance.
&ldquo;Pack quickly. Leave your key.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Madam.&rdquo;
Sonia did exactly that. She packed the few things she had brought, removed every trace of herself that was not already false, left the key where instructed, and went out through the servant entrance with one modest bag in her hand and the drive hidden close against her body.
Only when the taxi pulled away did she let herself look back.
The villa stood calm behind its gates, beautiful and controlled, the sort of place that made its own philosophy look inevitable.
At the airport she expected only relief.
Instead she found the next stage waiting for her under soft lights and polished signage.
The display stood just beyond security retail, branded with the quiet assurance of Mirende Ginciao. Two female mannequins faced the concourse in fitted coats and gloves. The first wore a half-mask in pale cream, shaped neatly over the mouth and nose and held by elegant side fittings that made it look almost medical until the eye adjusted and saw the intention beneath the styling. The placard called the line Jinshuwan. Hygienic. Refined. Public-minded.
The second mannequin wore something worse.
White porcelain glaze. Smooth cheeks. Only the eyes left open. The lower face sealed behind a polished shell so beautiful it took Sonia a second to register it as captivity. Beside it, under glass, lay the internal mouthpiece that anchored the thing in place.
Jurenmuse, the placard said.
Luxury silence.
Sonia stood still in the flow of passengers and felt cold move through her body from throat to stomach.
This was why Cheng had cared about elegance in the conference room. Why he had spoken about aspiration instead of punishment. The mask did not announce itself as violence. It announced itself as refinement, health, composure, social grace. Something a proper woman might choose because choice had already been bent into the shape they wanted.
An attendant beside the display adjusted a brochure and smiled at two women who stopped to look.
&ldquo;The lighter line is ideal for daily wear,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Very comfortable. Very modern.&rdquo;
One of the women touched the sample with gloved fingers.
&ldquo;And the porcelain?&rdquo;
&ldquo;For formal use,&rdquo; the attendant said. &ldquo;For women who value complete presentation.&rdquo;
Sonia moved away before they could see her expression.
She had spent days in a house where women were made invisible through service, posture, and obedience. Now she was looking at the next turn of the screw. Not only disciplined women. Silent women. Women rendered beautiful, useful, and unreadable from the mouth down.
The knowledge sat in her phone like heat.
By the time she reached her gate, relief had curdled into urgency.
She was not carrying home a theory anymore.
She was carrying evidence, a glimpse of intention, and the sick understanding that the world ahead would be gentler in language and harsher in fact than she had feared.

Return from Shanghai# Sonia came back to Geneva at sunset and knew before she reached passport control that the city had moved on without her.
It was there in the posture of the women ahead of her in line, in the practiced way they managed luggage with gloved hands and short, contained movements, in the men beside them who stepped in without discussion to lift, carry, direct, decide. Two months were not long enough to rebuild a city from the ground up. Long enough, apparently, to make what would once have looked theatrical start passing for ordinary.
By the time she reached her apartment she was too tired to do anything useful with the data from Cheng&rsquo;s household. She showered, stood too long under hot water, then lay awake in her own bed with Shanghai still pressed into her body. Her shoulders kept trying to settle into the set the corset had taught them. When she turned on her side, she missed the rigid certainty of being laced.
That bothered her enough to keep her awake another hour.
In the morning she called Anna.
The sound of her friend&rsquo;s voice hurt more than Sonia had expected.
&ldquo;Sonia?&rdquo;
Warm. Bright. Glad to hear her.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m back.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Oh, good. Are you well?&rdquo;
No, Sonia thought. Not in any useful sense.
&ldquo;Tired. Can we meet?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Of course. Lakeside Café?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
The walk there gave her too much time to look.
Geneva had not become Shanghai. It had become itself more decisively. The women on the streets were still less uniform, less overtly stylized, but the direction of travel was obvious now. Corsets showed under tailored jackets. Skirts narrowed. Heels clicked in shorter rhythms. Men held doors, set hands at waists and elbows, steered women gently through crossings and into trams. Sonia passed a couple in front of a bakery: the woman, elegant and blonde and visibly over-dressed for buying bread, stood with her hands folded while the man chose for both of them. She looked calm. Pleased, even. That was the part Sonia could not stop tripping over. Not misery. Not humiliation performed under protest. Settlement.
The Lakeside Café had kept its awning, its polished windows, its view of the water. Inside, the balance had shifted. More structured women. More lowered gazes. More little pauses before women sat, stood, spoke, reached. Sonia felt it before she consciously counted it. The room ran differently now.
She saw Anna at once and still had to stop for half a second before walking over.
Anna was dressed for afternoon rather than display, but that only made the change harder to dismiss. Dark fitted jacket. Pale high-collared blouse. Gloves laid neatly beside her cup. A hat pinned with understated care. Her corset set her upright in a way that no longer looked imposed from the outside. It looked inhabited. She sat with a composure Sonia had once associated with very old paintings of very expensive women who had never been allowed to slump in their lives.
Then Anna looked up and smiled, and for a moment the years before the virus came flooding back so hard Sonia almost hated her for it.
&ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo;
She rose carefully. Sonia stepped in and let herself be kissed once on the cheek.
&ldquo;Hello.&rdquo;
Anna drew back just far enough to look at her properly.
&ldquo;You&rsquo;ve lost weight.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Shanghai food wasn&rsquo;t the problem.&rdquo;
That made Anna&rsquo;s mouth soften with concern.
&ldquo;Sit down, please.&rdquo;
Sonia sat. The waiter appeared almost immediately. Anna did not order for her, thank God, but there was still something Changed in the way she handled the interaction: not submissive exactly, because he was only a waiter, but measured, composed, polished down to the smallest movement.
When he was gone Anna folded her hands lightly on the table.
&ldquo;You look exhausted.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I am.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Was it useful?&rdquo;
Sonia looked at her for a second.
Useful.
She had infiltrated a house built around feminine service, stolen evidence from a man who spoke about social coercion like a product rollout, watched the prototype of female silence polished into luxury goods, and come home carrying enough data to ruin lives if anyone believed her.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;More useful than I wanted it to be.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes sharpened with interest, but before Sonia could start, Anna&rsquo;s expression changed. Something gentler moved through it. Anticipation. Pleasure.
&ldquo;I have news too.&rdquo;
Of course you do, Sonia thought. Of course the city kept moving while I was playing maid in somebody else&rsquo;s nightmare.
&ldquo;Good news?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s smile deepened.
&ldquo;Very.&rdquo;
She reached into her bag and produced a cream card edged in dark green. Heavy paper. Formal lettering. Sonia did not need to open it to know what it was, but Anna waited until she had.
Julien Moreau requests the honor&hellip;
The giving-away ceremony.
Next weekend.
Jade Garden.
Sonia looked back up.
Anna was watching her with open happiness and something more vulnerable under it, the hope of being answered kindly.
&ldquo;You&rsquo;re inviting me.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I very much want you there.&rdquo;
There were a dozen things Sonia might have said. This is insane. You sound like somebody describing her own auction. Do you hear yourself when you say giving-away like that, with that expression on your face?
Instead she set the card down carefully.
&ldquo;Tell me.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s relief was small but visible.
&ldquo;It will be simple. My father will place me formally in Julien&rsquo;s care. There will be tea afterward, and only a few people. I wanted it to feel&hellip; quiet. Right.&rdquo;
Quiet. Right.
Words Sonia could not stop hearing the way Anna meant them, not the way Sonia did.
&ldquo;And that&rsquo;s what this feels like to you?&rdquo;
Anna held her gaze.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
No embarrassment. No defensive edge. No sense that she had to justify herself to survive the conversation. She was not parroting. She was sincere.
&ldquo;I know how it sounds to you,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;Or I think I do. But it isn&rsquo;t degradation, Sonia. It feels like being settled. Properly. I don&rsquo;t know how else to explain it without making you angry.&rdquo;
That landed more cleanly than any speech could have. Anna still knew her well enough to predict the shape of the argument and gentle it in advance.
Sonia wrapped both hands around her cup, though the coffee had already started cooling.
&ldquo;Try anyway.&rdquo;
Anna smiled a little, sadly this time.
&ldquo;All right. Julien takes care of me. He pays attention. He notices when something is too much, or when I need help, or when I am pretending not to need help because I want to feel proud. Father did that when I was younger in a different way. This is&hellip; a continuation, but more intimate. More chosen. I don&rsquo;t feel erased by it.&rdquo;
Sonia thought of Cheng saying women adapted first in the body, then in habit, then in memory.
She thought of the women in the airport, the subway, the villa, the boutiques.
She thought of Anna sitting opposite her in perfect posture, speaking about transfer and care with luminous calm.
&ldquo;You really are happy.&rdquo;
Anna blinked, as if that should not have been in question.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
The grief of it came strangely. Not as shock. The shock was long gone. This was smaller and meaner. The grief of realizing your friend had crossed somewhere you could still reach her physically but not morally, not without tearing something open in her that she would call healing.
&ldquo;Sonia?&rdquo;
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m here.&rdquo;
Anna hesitated. Then, very softly: &ldquo;Will you come?&rdquo;
Affection, disbelief, strategy, sorrow, all of it tightened together under Sonia&rsquo;s ribs. She wanted to refuse on principle. She wanted to say yes because Anna was asking with that hopeful carefulness and because refusing would wound her. She wanted to go because this ceremony was evidence, culture turning itself into ritual in real time. She wanted to go because she could not stop looking at the cliff edge and asking how far over her friend had gone.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said at last. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll come.&rdquo;
Anna exhaled as if she had been holding her breath.
&ldquo;Thank you.&rdquo;
The gratitude in her voice was intimate enough to make Sonia feel cheap.
&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t have to thank me for attending your transfer from one man to another.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s cheeks colored faintly.
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
Then, because she was Anna and because the virus had changed her without making her stupid, she added, &ldquo;But I am grateful anyway.&rdquo;
That almost made Sonia laugh.
It also made the next part harder.
&ldquo;I didn&rsquo;t go to Shanghai for academic curiosity,&rdquo; Sonia said.
Anna went still.
&ldquo;I assumed that much.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I got into Cheng&rsquo;s household.&rdquo;
For the first time since Sonia sat down, Anna&rsquo;s composure cracked. Not dramatically. Just enough for the old Anna to show through in the sharpness of her attention.
&ldquo;You what?&rdquo;
&ldquo;As a maid.&rdquo;
Anna stared at her.
&ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;As a maid.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
The waiter arrived with fresh hot water and retreated again under the temperature of the silence between them.
Anna lowered her voice.
&ldquo;That was dangerous.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I noticed.&rdquo;
There it was, that flicker of the woman Sonia had missed: dry, intelligent, a little horrified, fully present.
She told Anna enough of it to matter. The household. Cheng. The lab. The files. Not every detail of what service had felt like in her body, not the dangerous softness of slipping into usefulness, but enough to make clear that this was real and that she had brought something back.
Anna listened without interrupting until Sonia mentioned the genomic annotations and the behavioral layering notes.
&ldquo;You have them?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Copied. Encrypted. Backed up in three places.&rdquo;
Anna sat back slowly.
&ldquo;Sonia&hellip;&rdquo;
She looked shaken now, though not in the way Sonia had once wanted. Not outraged at the whole social order. Not torn loose from her new life by revelation. The scientist in her had woken first. Sonia could see it happen.
&ldquo;If those files are genuine,&rdquo; Anna said, &ldquo;they could tell us how the adaptive layers are organized. Not just symptom expression. Memory architecture. Reward pathways. Possibly the sex-differentiated compulsion design.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s why I&rsquo;m here.&rdquo;
Anna lowered her eyes to the table for a second, and when she looked up again Sonia saw the conflict arrive. Not between helping and refusing, exactly. Something subtler. The pull of duty in two directions that no longer felt separate to her.
&ldquo;You want me to examine the data.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
Anna was quiet.
Sonia knew what she was really asking. Use the part of you the virus left brilliant. Use the part it made obedient. Use the way service now reaches straight into your pleasure centers and turns usefulness into emotional truth. Help me exploit what was done to you so I can maybe undo some part of it.
The thought made her stomach turn.
&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t have to answer now,&rdquo; Sonia said. &ldquo;I know this is—&rdquo;
&ldquo;I want to help.&rdquo;
The words came cleanly, without hesitation.
Sonia stopped.
Anna went on, gentler now.
&ldquo;If there is a way to understand it better, I want to help. If there is a way to reduce harm, all the more. I would not refuse you that, Sonia.&rdquo;
Would not refuse you.
The phrasing hit exactly where Sonia had feared it would.
&ldquo;Are you helping because you want to know,&rdquo; Sonia asked carefully, &ldquo;or because helping someone you care about now feels&hellip; compulsory?&rdquo;
Anna did not flinch.
&ldquo;Both, probably.&rdquo;
Honest. Calm. Almost unbearably honest.
&ldquo;The second part doesn&rsquo;t make the first untrue.&rdquo;
Sonia looked down at the invitation card beside her cup. Jade Garden. Giving-away ceremony. Formal transfer. Then back at Anna, who was offering her a cure with the same sincere readiness she would probably offer tea, mending, or her own body if the world asked for it in the right tone.
This was useful.
This was intimate.
This was morally filthy.
And she was going to do it anyway.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; Sonia said.
&ldquo;Bring me the files,&rdquo; Anna replied. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll start as soon as I can. Julien will understand if I tell him I am assisting with something medically important.&rdquo;
There it was again, that easy movement through permission and dependence. Even her offer of professional help came already fitted to the structure of her new life.
Sonia hated that she noticed the tenderness in it.
&ldquo;Thank you,&rdquo; she said, and heard how tired she sounded.
Anna&rsquo;s expression softened.
&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t need to thank me.&rdquo;
The faintest smile touched Sonia&rsquo;s mouth.
&ldquo;Apparently everybody does now.&rdquo;
That won her a small laugh, real and surprised and achingly familiar.
For a few seconds they were only themselves again, or near enough to make the loss hurt.
Then the moment passed. The café remained what it had become. The lake shone beyond the windows. Around them women sat in fitted jackets and corsets, rising carefully when men approached the table, lowering their eyes when spoken to, smiling as if this had always been how peace was meant to look.
When they stood to leave, Anna touched Sonia&rsquo;s hand.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m glad you&rsquo;re home.&rdquo;
Sonia looked at her, at the serene posture, the careful clothes, the happiness she could not dismiss as fake.
&ldquo;I know,&rdquo; she said.
It was the truest answer she had.

Decoding The Virus# Anna sent for Sonia late in the evening with a message so brief it made her hurry.
Come tonight. I have enough to show you.
By the time Sonia let herself into Anna&rsquo;s underground lab, Anna was already there in a dark skirt and blouse, obviously corseted, but sleeves rolled neatly to the forearms, mask hanging from one wrist because she was alone below ground and had apparently forgotten to put it back on after drinking. The sight of her bare face bent over a screen hit Sonia with a strange force. Scientist first. Changed woman second. No, not second. Both at once.
Anna looked up at once and smiled.
&ldquo;You&rsquo;re here.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You said you had something.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I do.&rdquo; Anna pushed a strand of hair back behind her ear with the back of her wrist, distracted, bright-eyed, almost feverish with work. &ldquo;More than something, I think.&rdquo;
The lab bench had been taken over by printouts, sequence maps, notes in Anna&rsquo;s small sharp handwriting, and three separate screens glowing with layered genetic data Sonia could not begin to parse at a glance. On one tray sat two untouched cups of coffee Anna had apparently poured hours earlier and forgotten.
Sonia crossed to the central workstation.
&ldquo;Tell me.&rdquo;
Anna breathed in once, steadying herself not from nerves but from the effort of compressing too much thought into speech.
&ldquo;The viral genome is not behaving like a normal genome at all. Not in proportion. Not in intent. Most of it is payload.&rdquo; She touched the nearest screen. &ldquo;It carries just enough machinery to infect, cross the blood-brain barrier, survive, replicate, and distribute its instructions. After that it becomes something closer to compressed architecture.&rdquo;
Sonia looked from the screen to Anna.
&ldquo;Architecture for what.&rdquo;
&ldquo;For people.&rdquo;
The answer came without hesitation.
Anna clicked to the next view, where colored regions spread across the display in dense bands and nested annotations.
&ldquo;This section here is broad-spectrum. It appears in everyone. Knowledge packets, or something very close to them. Language acquisition. Skill clusters. Habit scaffolding. I can&rsquo;t map every piece yet, but it explains too much too neatly to be anything else.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Chinese.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Chinese, yes. Showyou. Domestic skills in some subjects. Professional confidence in others. Fine motor adjustments. Social scripts.&rdquo; Anna gave a brief helpless little gesture with one hand. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s staggeringly efficient.&rdquo;
Sonia heard the admiration and felt her jaw tighten.
&ldquo;Anna.&rdquo;
Anna stopped.
&ldquo;I know,&rdquo; she said quietly. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m not forgetting what it is. Let me finish.&rdquo;
That took some of the edge out of Sonia&rsquo;s anger. Not much. Enough.
Anna turned back to the screen.
&ldquo;The next layer is where it becomes uglier. Memory integration. It doesn&rsquo;t simply install behavior. It edits context. It folds the new values into existing personal history and then supplies enough false continuity to make the result feel native.&rdquo;
She brought up another set of notes.
&ldquo;That explains the rituals. The certainty. Why Changed people remember rules, traditions, etiquette, gender expectations, all of it, as if they had always grown up inside them. The virus isn&rsquo;t merely making them obey. It&rsquo;s giving the brain a past that makes obedience feel like recognition.&rdquo;
Sonia leaned both hands on the edge of the bench.
&ldquo;So when Elena says women should be seen and not heard—&rdquo;
&ldquo;That may be an implanted maternal memory. Or an edited one. Or a genuine memory reweighted until it becomes foundational.&rdquo; Anna met her eyes. &ldquo;The point is that the brain no longer experiences it as foreign.&rdquo;
That landed hard enough that Sonia had to look away for a moment.
The fluorescent light reflected faintly in the metal surface of the sink. Somewhere behind the walls a pump clicked on and off.
&ldquo;And the compulsions?&rdquo;
Anna nodded and went to the third display.
&ldquo;Sex-differentiated.&rdquo;
She said it the way a mathematician might say inevitable.
&ldquo;The virus carries common material for everyone, but the behavioral enforcement branches. Female pathways are shaped toward obedience, service, modesty, dependence, feminization, and reward-linked submission. Male pathways toward authority, protectiveness, management, supervision, responsibility.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Patriarchy,&rdquo; Sonia said flatly.
Anna&rsquo;s mouth softened with something almost apologetic.
&ldquo;Engineered patriarchy, yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good. I just wanted to hear you say it.&rdquo;
Anna did not flinch.
&ldquo;It is. That doesn&rsquo;t make the design less remarkable.&rdquo;
There it was again. The split. The thing Sonia kept coming up against whenever Anna worked on the data too long: moral clarity in one room of her mind, aesthetic and scientific rapture in the next, both real, neither strong enough to cancel the other.
&ldquo;You admire it.&rdquo;
Anna looked down at the printout in her hand as if the paper itself deserved honesty.
&ldquo;In part.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Christ.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo; Anna set the paper down carefully. &ldquo;If someone built a cathedral over a mass grave, the grave would still be a horror. The architecture might still be extraordinary.&rdquo;
Sonia stared at her.
&ldquo;That is an appalling comparison.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;It is. It&rsquo;s also the closest thing I have to the truth.&rdquo;
For a few seconds neither of them spoke.
The lab hummed. Anna&rsquo;s forgotten coffee sat cooling beside the keyboard. Sonia felt the weight of the city above them, all those women on trams and in cafés and offices and shops moving through structures they remembered as normal because something microscopic had gone into their brains and taught them how to welcome the cage.
&ldquo;Can it be undone?&rdquo; she asked at last.
Anna exhaled slowly.
&ldquo;Not cleanly. Not yet. Maybe not fully.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s stomach dropped.
Anna lifted a hand before she could speak.
&ldquo;But that isn&rsquo;t the same as impossible.&rdquo;
She tapped a highlighted band in the genome map.
&ldquo;The payload is segmented. Not elegantly separated, but segmented. Whoever made it expected modular expression. Some clusters appear conditional. Some clearly interact with pre-existing physiology before activating. Some are layered over others in a way that suggests timing, thresholds, reinforcement.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Meaning?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Meaning if we understand how the instructions are structured, we may be able to interfere with parts of the program without having to unravel the whole virus at once.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Memory first?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Maybe. Or reward structures. Or sex-specific compulsion cascades. I don&rsquo;t know yet.&rdquo;
Anna reached for a second printout and handed it across. Sonia took it, though most of it meant nothing to her beyond the red circles and notes.
&ldquo;This is the part that concerns you personally,&rdquo; Anna said.
Sonia looked up.
&ldquo;Me.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Your immunity isn&rsquo;t complete immunity. That&rsquo;s the important distinction.&rdquo; Anna came around to her side of the bench so they were looking at the same page. &ldquo;You received the knowledge layer. The social patterning. Some of the physical response scaffolding. You understand too much too intuitively for anything else to be true.&rdquo;
Sonia thought of Shanghai. The maid training. The tram. The instant curtsy to the man who offered her a seat.
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;But the coercive part misfired.&rdquo; Anna pointed to a small region of notation. &ldquo;The virus binds to a target protein before one of the female compulsion cascades fully activates. In you, it seems to have attached to a neighboring structure with enough similarity to begin the process and enough difference to stop it completing correctly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;So I got the language of the cage without the lock.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes flickered.
&ldquo;Something like that.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And if it had hit the right target?&rdquo;
Anna did not answer immediately.
&ldquo;You would feel less divided.&rdquo;
The quietness of it was worse than if she had sounded triumphant.
Sonia set the printout down.
&ldquo;Less divided.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You mean I would want all this.&rdquo;
Anna folded her hands together, thinking before she spoke.
&ldquo;I think you already want parts of it.&rdquo;
That went through Sonia like a slap because it was too close to something she had been trying not to name.
&ldquo;Anna.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You asked for honesty.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Not psychoanalysis.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t psychoanalysis.&rdquo; Anna&rsquo;s voice stayed very gentle. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s pattern recognition.&rdquo;
Sonia turned away and walked two steps toward the sink, then back again because there was nowhere useful to put the surge of anger. The room was too small for pacing and too bright for hiding.
&ldquo;Fine,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;What do we actually do with this.&rdquo;
Anna, mercifully, shifted with her.
&ldquo;We keep going. I refine the map. I identify the memory-editing structures more clearly. I isolate the female and male enforcement bands as far as I can. We test hypotheses against expression patterns, not just sequence predictions. If I can get enough time with the analyzer downstairs at GAIA without questions being asked, I can accelerate some of it.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Can you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Probably.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Probably is not my favorite word this month.&rdquo;
Anna smiled despite herself.
&ldquo;Mine either.&rdquo;
Sonia looked back at the screens.
For the first time since Shanghai, the data in front of her felt like more than horror and theory. It was still horror. It was still theory. But it had edges now. Structure. Places where a hand might pry.
&ldquo;One genetic sequence at a time,&rdquo; she said.
Anna&rsquo;s expression sharpened with interest.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You think that&rsquo;s possible.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I think it may be the only sane way to approach it.&rdquo;
Sonia let out a breath she had not realized she was holding.
&ldquo;All right.&rdquo;
Anna picked up her cup, remembered only after lifting it that the coffee would be cold, and put it down again.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; she echoed.
Then, more softly: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m glad you brought this to me.&rdquo;
That was the danger too. Anna meant it in every register at once. As a scientist. As a friend. As a Changed woman to whom service had become an avenue toward joy.
Sonia looked at her and felt the same complicated gratitude and grief she always felt now.
&ldquo;So am I,&rdquo; she said.
It was true.

The Vial# They met in a privacy cabin at the Lakeside Café because neither of them felt like performing this conversation in public.
By then the cabins had lost any sense of novelty. Women stepped in and out of them between errands, meetings, lunches, coffees, adjustments. The city had folded female concealment into infrastructure with insulting speed. Sonia still hated that.
Inside the cabin she removed her mask and set it on the table between them. Anna did the same, more gracefully, then smoothed her headscarf back into place and reached for her satchel.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve gone further,&rdquo; she said.
Sonia did not touch the tea the waiter had just brought.
&ldquo;Into which part.&rdquo;
&ldquo;All of it, unfortunately.&rdquo;
Anna took out a thin stack of notes, then another, then a small sealed sample bag with a labeled vial inside. Sonia&rsquo;s eyes went to the vial at once and stayed there just long enough for Anna to notice.
&ldquo;Later,&rdquo; Anna said softly. &ldquo;First the rest.&rdquo;
Sonia sat back.
&ldquo;Fine.&rdquo;
Anna arranged the papers in front of her with the focused tidiness Sonia had known since university. Some habits survived anything.
&ldquo;The virus is bigger in intent than we thought.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Bigger than engineered patriarchy and memory rewriting.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
There was no triumph in the answer. That made it worse.
&ldquo;The behavioral program isn&rsquo;t only personal. It&rsquo;s civilizational.&rdquo; Anna tapped one of the pages. &ldquo;Consumption suppression. Reward-linked minimalism. Reduced appetite for accumulation. Increased tolerance for regulation where it can be framed as communal necessity. Stronger compliance with environmental restrictions. More hostility to hyperwealth and extractive industry.&rdquo;
Sonia stared at her.
&ldquo;So Cheng built a plague to solve capitalism.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s mouth twitched in reluctant amusement.
&ldquo;That is a grotesque summary.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Is it inaccurate?&rdquo;
Anna considered, then shook her head once.
&ldquo;Not entirely.&rdquo;
For a moment the absurdity of it almost made Sonia laugh. Then it settled back into horror. Cheng had not merely wanted obedient women and supervisory men. He had wanted a whole species reordered around interdependence, managed desire, sustainable limits, collective pressure. The patriarchy was not a side effect. It was one structural choice inside a larger redesign.
&ldquo;Jesus.&rdquo;
Anna lowered her gaze to the notes.
&ldquo;Yes. Well, Confucius would be closer.&rdquo;
Sonia looked out through the frosted panel of the cabin door where blurred bodies moved past in softened silhouettes.
&ldquo;And people call this harmony.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Because it often feels like harmony from the inside.&rdquo;
Anna said it simply. No shame. No performance. That honesty was one of the few things Sonia still trusted absolutely in her.
&ldquo;You really think that makes it better.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo; Anna lifted her eyes. &ldquo;I think it makes it more difficult to fight honestly.&rdquo;
That shut Sonia up for a second.
Anna turned a page.
&ldquo;Your anomaly is clearer now too.&rdquo;
There it was. The other reason for the meeting. The thing sitting under everything else.
&ldquo;Tell me.&rdquo;
&ldquo;The female compulsion structure should have locked to a receptor family with very narrow specificity.&rdquo; Anna touched a marked line of sequence. &ldquo;In you, binding occurred adjacent to the intended target. Close enough for knowledge transfer. Close enough for social and behavioral patterning. Close enough for some embodied adaptation. Not close enough for full compulsive reinforcement.&rdquo;
Sonia held still.
&ldquo;So I got enough to understand the system and perform inside it, but not enough to stop resisting it.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Lucky me.&rdquo;
Anna winced very slightly.
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t think it feels lucky from where you&rsquo;re sitting.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; Sonia said. &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t.&rdquo;
Anna folded her hands.
&ldquo;Sonia, I need you to understand something before I say the rest.&rdquo;
That got Sonia&rsquo;s attention in a sharper way.
&ldquo;All right.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I am not saying this because I want you easier to manage. Or because I think dissent is ugly. Or because I want you less yourself.&rdquo;
Sonia felt her body tense anyway.
&ldquo;Anna.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Let me finish.&rdquo;
The old note in her voice did it. Scientist, yes. Changed woman, yes. But also Anna asking for a full hearing because what came next mattered and she wanted to be fair.
Sonia gave a short nod.
Anna reached for the sample bag and slid it across the table.
The vial inside was very small. Almost insultingly small for something that could alter a life.
&ldquo;I can complete the lock.&rdquo;
Sonia looked at the bag, then back at Anna.
&ldquo;You can what.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Not redesign the virus. Not rebuild you from scratch. But the gap is narrow enough that I can bridge it.&rdquo;
Every word after that seemed to arrive too clearly.
&ldquo;I isolated the missing activation pathway from the sequence expression models and built a supplemental vector.&rdquo; Anna&rsquo;s voice had gone low, careful. &ldquo;Or the beginning of one. It would not infect you with the whole virus again. It would trigger what failed to trigger the first time.&rdquo;
Sonia did not touch the bag.
&ldquo;You made me a dose.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I made you a possible cure.&rdquo;
That word was almost enough to make Sonia angry all over again.
&ldquo;Cure.&rdquo;
&ldquo;For the conflict.&rdquo;
Sonia laughed once, without humor.
&ldquo;So that&rsquo;s what I am now. In conflict.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You are,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;Painfully.&rdquo;
The cruelty of that was that it came from pity, not contempt.
Anna went on before Sonia could turn the moment into something theatrical.
&ldquo;You know the language of the system. You feel parts of it in your body. You adapt to it socially. You are susceptible to its rewards without being able to settle into them. Every day you live half inside something you cannot fully inhabit and half outside a world that no longer exists. That isn&rsquo;t freedom, Sonia. It&rsquo;s a permanent abrasion.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s fingers curled around her untouched teacup hard enough to make the porcelain click softly against the saucer.
&ldquo;And if I swallow that.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes moved to the bag between them.
&ldquo;The female compulsion pathway would activate properly. The friction would stop.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I would become obedient.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Dependent.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Happy.&rdquo;
Anna did not answer at once.
&ldquo;Very likely,&rdquo; she said finally.
There was the terror. Not brain death. Not slavery in obvious chains. Relief.
Sonia looked at the vial again. It was so small. A mouthful of water. Two hours, maybe. Then no more split self. No more putting on the corset each morning and asking whether the comfort meant anything. No more tram rides where approval felt good enough to shame her. No more mask, no more city, no more Henry, no more Anna, all of it pulling at parts of her she had never wanted named.
Only peace.
Or surrender so complete it learned to call itself peace and never looked back.
&ldquo;You really believe this would help me.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression changed then. Softer. Almost unbearably tender.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t hear how frightening that is.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I do.&rdquo; Anna reached across the table, then stopped before touching Sonia&rsquo;s hand, giving her room to refuse. &ldquo;But I also know what it feels like to stop fighting yourself.&rdquo;
Sonia stared at the space between their hands.
&ldquo;That isn&rsquo;t what this would be.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; Anna said. &ldquo;Not exactly.&rdquo;
For a second neither of them moved.
Then Sonia said, &ldquo;Do you ever hear yourself? Do you ever hear how loving you sound when you offer to finish a virus that turned women into wards.&rdquo;
Anna went very still.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said after a moment. &ldquo;And I know how that sounds to you too.&rdquo;
The answer was so level, so unguarded, that Sonia felt her anger falter against it.
Anna was not trying to win an argument.
She was trying to help.
That was the unbearable part.
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to be fixed,&rdquo; Sonia said.
Anna gave the smallest nod.
&ldquo;All right.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I mean it.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I want a way to fight this.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know that too.&rdquo;
The cabin had gone quiet except for the muffled life of the café outside and the faint clink of cups from somewhere beyond the wall.
Anna withdrew her hand and sat back.
&ldquo;Take it anyway.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s eyes snapped up.
&ldquo;What.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Take it.&rdquo; Anna pushed the bag the last inch across the table. &ldquo;Not because I expect you to use it. Because I know there may come a day when the conflict becomes too much, and if that day comes, I would rather the choice be yours than somebody else&rsquo;s.&rdquo;
That was almost worse than if Anna had pleaded.
&ldquo;You&rsquo;re giving me the thing that would finish what Cheng started.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m giving you an exit from being divided, if you ever decide division is no longer survivable.&rdquo;
Sonia looked at the bag for a long time.
Then she picked it up.
The plastic crackled softly in her hand. The vial inside looked harmless. Transparent. Clean.
&ldquo;I hate that you made this.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I hate that part of me wants to know what it would feel like.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s face softened with something too close to compassion for comfort.
&ldquo;I know that too.&rdquo;
Sonia put the bag into her satchel and felt its presence there at once, absurdly weighty for something so light.
The rest of the conversation moved around it. They returned, because they had to, to the technical work. Anna clarified a few points in the sequence maps. Sonia asked better questions now, sharper ones, partly because the vial had terrified her into wanting useful information and partly because she needed to get away from the bag at her side before she did something stupid like take it back out and keep looking at it.
Then they were finished.
Anna remasked first. Sonia followed.
The mouthpiece settled into place with the same degrading precision as always, and for one terrible second Sonia thought about how easy it would be to stop hating that if she let herself.
Outside the cabin, the café received them as two proper women returning to public view.
Anna touched her wrist in farewell.
Sonia carried the vial home in her bag and felt it there every step of the way.

Sonia Does Not Want To Be Alone# Sonia left the vial in the back of her refrigerator behind a jar of mustard and half a bottle of white wine she had been meaning to throw out for months.
That did not make it less present.
The knowledge of it sat in the apartment like another person. Every time she opened the fridge she saw the little glass promise tucked in the corner and felt the same nauseating pull. Relief. Defeat. Curiosity. She stopped opening the refrigerator unless she absolutely had to.
Three days later she was back in a privacy cabin with Anna.
Not because she had changed her mind about the vial.
Because she had begun to realize that the vial was not the only answer the city was offering her.
Anna had already removed her mask by the time Sonia entered. She sat with tea and a plate of sliced fruit untouched in front of her, posture still perfect even inside the hidden little room where women were allowed to unfasten themselves enough to eat. Sonia locked the cabin door, set down her bag, and pulled her own mask off with more force than elegance.
Anna noticed at once.
&ldquo;Bad day?&rdquo;
Sonia sat.
&ldquo;Clarifying day.&rdquo;
Anna waited.
That was one of the things Sonia both loved and hated now. Anna had become very good at waiting in a way that made the other person either speak honestly or hear herself refusing to.
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to be alone,&rdquo; Sonia said.
The words changed the air between them.
Anna did not look surprised. Only gentler.
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; she said softly. &ldquo;I didn&rsquo;t think you did.&rdquo;
Sonia let out a short breath.
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s infuriating.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Probably.&rdquo;
Outside the cabin, faint through the walls, came the muted life of the café: cups set down, footsteps, a burst of laughter cut short by the closed little world they were sitting in.
Sonia looked at her tea instead of at Anna.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve spent months telling myself the problem was ideology. Coercion. The city. The clothes. The rules. And it is those things.&rdquo; She rubbed one thumb against the side of her cup. &ldquo;But that isn&rsquo;t the whole problem.&rdquo;
Anna remained quiet.
&ldquo;The whole problem,&rdquo; Sonia said, &ldquo;is that public life is becoming easier than private life.&rdquo;
That got a visible reaction. Not triumph. Recognition.
&ldquo;Go on.&rdquo;
Sonia almost told her to go to hell for sounding so calm, but the calm was exactly why she had come.
&ldquo;At work, in the street, on the tram, in shops, everywhere, someone is there. Someone notices. Someone adjusts, helps, anticipates, makes space. It&rsquo;s the men around us, and I hate how much I notice that.&rdquo; She glanced up at Anna at last. &ldquo;Then I come home and it&rsquo;s just me and the mask in the drawer and the corset on the chair and that ridiculous little vial in the fridge, and the apartment feels&hellip;&rdquo;
She stopped.
Anna did not rescue her from the sentence.
&ldquo;Empty?&rdquo; Anna offered after a moment.
&ldquo;Wrong,&rdquo; Sonia said. &ldquo;Too quiet. Like all the scaffolding disappears and I have to hold myself up alone again.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression softened with painful understanding.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
That yes came from too far inside the life Sonia was trying to understand.
&ldquo;And before you say it, no, I am not asking whether swallowing your science project would fix it.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s mouth curved, just a little.
&ldquo;I wasn&rsquo;t going to say that.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Liar.&rdquo;
&ldquo;A little.&rdquo;
It almost made Sonia smile.
Almost.
&ldquo;What I am asking,&rdquo; she said, &ldquo;is whether there is any version of this world where the answer isn&rsquo;t just total surrender to the virus.&rdquo;
Anna leaned back slightly.
&ldquo;You mean relationally.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I mean as a person. With another person. Without having to drink anything out of a vial because I&rsquo;m lonely.&rdquo;
Anna let the words settle before answering.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;There is.&rdquo;
Sonia felt something inside her ease and tighten at once.
&ldquo;Then explain it to me like I&rsquo;m not a tourist in your religion.&rdquo;
Anna laughed very softly at that, then folded her hands.
&ldquo;All right. But it isn&rsquo;t a religion.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That sounded exactly like something someone in a religion would say.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sonia.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Fine. Go on.&rdquo;
Anna took a sip of tea.
&ldquo;The first thing is that you keep treating all of this as if the only options are total autonomy or total external control. That isn&rsquo;t how it feels from inside a good relationship here.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good according to whom.&rdquo;
&ldquo;According to the woman living in it.&rdquo;
That was fair enough to irritate Sonia.
Anna continued before she could pounce on the phrasing.
&ldquo;Yes, there is hierarchy. Yes, a Changed man expects obedience. Yes, he may correct, direct, manage, and structure parts of a woman&rsquo;s life. None of that is trivial. But courtship exists because women still need to know what kind of man they are being asked to trust with all that power.&rdquo;
Sonia sat very still.
&ldquo;And what am I meant to be looking for.&rdquo;
Anna answered without hesitation.
&ldquo;How he handles power when he doesn&rsquo;t need to impress you.&rdquo;
That was the first sentence of the conversation that sounded less like theory and more like something earned.
&ldquo;Meaning.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Watch him with waitresses. Junior staff. Older women. Men beneath him. Men above him. Does he become showy when he can dominate? Does he become sulky when he can&rsquo;t? Does he remember what makes other people comfortable, or only what flatters him.&rdquo; Anna touched the table lightly with two fingertips. &ldquo;Does he notice your body as something he must manage responsibly, or only as something he wants.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s thoughts went to Henry so fast it almost embarrassed her.
Anna saw it.
Of course she saw it.
&ldquo;There is someone.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Possibly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Henry.&rdquo;
Sonia gave her a look.
&ldquo;You&rsquo;re insufferable.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You have signed his name three times in conversation this month and acted as if you weren&rsquo;t.&rdquo;
That shut Sonia up.
Anna waited just long enough to make her answer.
&ldquo;Fine. Henry.&rdquo;
The name sat between them differently once spoken plainly.
Anna&rsquo;s gaze warmed.
&ldquo;Tell me.&rdquo;
Sonia looked away first.
&ldquo;He keeps appearing.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That sounds ominous.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It is ominous. He&rsquo;s there when I need doors opened, files carried, chairs moved, books reached, logistics solved. He reads my signing faster than most men at work, and properly, not like he&rsquo;s waiting for speech to rescue it. He doesn&rsquo;t make a performance of helping. He just&hellip;&rdquo; She made a helpless motion with one hand. &ldquo;Fits himself into the gap before I have to ask.&rdquo;
Anna listened with maddening patience.
&ldquo;How does he treat other women?&rdquo;
&ldquo;The same way, more or less. Respectfully. Not flirtatiously. He doesn&rsquo;t get theatrical about it.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And men.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Well. Even when they&rsquo;re idiots.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Junior staff.&rdquo;
&ldquo;He&rsquo;s careful with them.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Staff in service roles.&rdquo;
Sonia thought of Henry thanking a cleaner for retrieving a dropped folder instead of letting her hover awkwardly until he noticed. Thought of him holding a lift door for two interns and stepping back so they entered first without comment.
&ldquo;Also well.&rdquo;
Anna nodded once, as if ticking something off an invisible list.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Good?&rdquo; Sonia echoed. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s all?&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo; Anna&rsquo;s mouth softened. &ldquo;Not all. How does he make you feel.&rdquo;
That was the question Sonia had been trying to avoid by turning Henry into a case study.
&ldquo;Supported,&rdquo; she said after a moment.
Anna said nothing.
&ldquo;Seen,&rdquo; Sonia added, quieter now.
Still Anna waited.
Sonia looked down at her hands.
&ldquo;Managed, a little,&rdquo; she admitted. &ldquo;And I don&rsquo;t know whether that alarms me or&hellip;&rdquo; She stopped.
&ldquo;Or relieves you.&rdquo;
Sonia met her eyes.
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
There was no point pretending otherwise in here.
Anna spoke carefully.
&ldquo;Then what you are looking at is not only politics or social pressure. It is temperament. Need. Desire. All the things you have spent a long time sorting into acceptable and unacceptable categories.&rdquo;
Sonia gave a small, humorless laugh.
&ldquo;You make it sound very tidy.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t tidy.&rdquo; Anna&rsquo;s voice dropped. &ldquo;It only becomes clearer when you stop lying to yourself about what feels good.&rdquo;
That one hurt because it was aimed well.
&ldquo;And if what feels good is the sort of thing feminist mothers warn their daughters about from birth?&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s gaze held hers.
&ldquo;Then perhaps feminist mothers are capable of being wrong about some daughters.&rdquo;
Sonia sat with that.
Not because it convinced her.
Because it did not bounce off.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; she said at last. &ldquo;Suppose I entertained this. Not the vial. Henry.&rdquo;
Anna nodded, serious again.
&ldquo;Then move slowly and pay attention.&rdquo;
&ldquo;To what.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Whether he makes space for your refusal.&rdquo;
That surprised Sonia.
&ldquo;A Changed man.&rdquo;
&ldquo;A good one, yes.&rdquo; Anna leaned forward slightly. &ldquo;Hierarchy here does not mean courtship begins with seizure. He should guide. Invite. Signal. Give you room to move toward him. If he crowds you too fast, if he wants obedience before trust, if he enjoys your uncertainty for its own sake, that matters.&rdquo;
Sonia thought about Henry again. The way he helped. The way he watched. The way he never quite pressed.
&ldquo;And correction.&rdquo;
Anna did not pretend not to understand the jump.
&ldquo;What about it.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You made it sound&hellip;&rdquo; Sonia searched for a word that did not sound adolescent. &ldquo;Structured.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It should be.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And humiliating.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s face colored faintly, but she did not look away.
&ldquo;Often.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You say that so calmly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Because panic would not make it less true.&rdquo;
Sonia hated how much she loved that answer.
Anna went on, gentler now.
&ldquo;Correction is one of the places where trust matters most. Outside observers imagine only punishment because that is the cleanest accusation. Inside a good relationship it is more specific than that. He should know what he is correcting, why, and what he wants from you after. Not simply obedience. Understanding. Realignment. If it becomes venting, if it becomes spectacle for its own sake, that is bad character, not good guardianship.&rdquo;
Sonia held her cup in both hands and let the heat sink into her palms.
&ldquo;You really do believe in this.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Even the humiliating parts.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s expression did something subtle then, more intimate than a blush and harder to look at directly.
&ldquo;Especially the parts that work,&rdquo; she said.
Silence followed that.
Not awkward silence.
The kind that comes when both people know too much about what the other has just confessed.
Sonia broke it first because she had to.
&ldquo;I am not promising anything.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I am not swallowing the vial.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I may still decide the whole system is obscene and Henry can stay in finance where he belongs.&rdquo;
That won her a laugh.
&ldquo;Also possible.&rdquo;
Sonia exhaled.
&ldquo;But I am going to watch him differently.&rdquo;
Anna nodded.
&ldquo;That sounds wise.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t sound so pleased.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I&rsquo;m your friend. I&rsquo;m allowed to be pleased when you stop pretending not to want things.&rdquo;
That could have sparked a fight on another day. Today it only left Sonia tired and oddly lighter.
When they left the cabin, they remasked in their separate practiced ways. Sonia&rsquo;s was still less elegant. Anna&rsquo;s was fluid now. Outside, the café folded them back into public femininity, porcelain and gloves and careful posture.
On the walk home Sonia stopped at a shoe shop, then at the chemist, then outside GAIA&rsquo;s darkened windows without entirely meaning to. Her reflection in the glass looked composed, proper, nearly serene from a distance.
Somewhere behind that reflection sat the vial in her refrigerator.
Somewhere ahead of it stood Henry in corridor doorways and bookshops and lifts, making room.
At home she took off her shoes, peeled off the gloves, and finally removed the mask.
Coming home, Sonia gets rid of her shoes and mask.
The apartment was quiet again.
Still quiet.
But now the quiet contained a shape she had been avoiding naming. Not abstract loneliness. Specific lack. No one to move the chair. No one to watch the line of her body and adjust the world around it. No one to receive the habits she had spent months learning.
She stood in front of the mirror for a long moment with the mask in one hand.
Then she set it down gently and thought of Henry.
Not as a theory.
Not as a symbol.
As a man she was going to allow herself to notice on purpose.

Anna and Julien&rsquo;s departure for London# Anna packed for London as if she were preparing for a long-promised education.
The suitcases lay open across the bedroom, Julien&rsquo;s side all practical order and folded shirts, hers a more complicated arrangement of underlayers, gloves, veils, and the first British purchases she had insisted on acquiring in advance. She stood between them in a pale house dress and half-mask, gesturing with bright quick hands while Julien sat on the edge of the bed and watched her enthusiasm sharpen itself into lists.
Anna standing amidst the luggage in their Geneva home.
Latex hoods for rain. Walking skirts. Capes with no arm slits. The hats, Sir. And the veils.
Julien smiled.
&ldquo;And the visible restraints.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s hands flickered faster.
Yes.
The sign carried so much delighted agreement that he laughed.
&ldquo;You have been studying.&rdquo;
I want to understand local standards before we arrive. I do not wish to look continental in the wrong way.
&ldquo;There are many wrong ways to look continental?&rdquo;
She paused just long enough for the joke to land, then signed with exaggerated dignity:
Apparently there are.
Julien set one of her folded scarves into the suitcase and watched her continue.
London excited her for reasons that reached further than fashion, though fashion provided the easiest vocabulary. Britain, from everything she had read, had taken the same Changed principles and pushed them outward into visible systems: hardware, public attachments, clothing that admitted dependence more openly, a city arranged to make female supervision seem both practical and elegant. Geneva still asked women to internalize the structure. London, as Anna imagined it, built the structure into walls, trains, weather gear, and streets.
The prospect thrilled her.
When they left for the station, she wore a charcoal travel dress over a corset and Yoyah Sheengzoh, cream gloves, a high-collared blouse, and a soft veiled travel hat she had insisted on trying in the British style for the journey. Julien carried the luggage. Anna kept one hand lightly on his sleeve and moved in the careful, shortened rhythm her underlayer required.
At the station the British carriage standards began before the train itself did.
Julien had reserved a first-plus compartment configured specifically for one man and one woman. His seat was broad, upholstered, and ordinary in a luxurious way. Beside it, where a second fauteuil might once have stood, was a kneeling pad with a fitted female travel position built into the floor and sidewall: belt points, posture supports, optional heel restraints, and a narrow display mounted at precisely the right angle for a collared woman whose head movement would be limited.
Anna stopped and looked at it with unveiled interest.
Julien noticed.
&ldquo;Too much?&rdquo;
Her hands answered immediately.
&ldquo;Not at all, Sir.&rdquo;
He seated her carefully, guiding the fall of her skirt and then fastening the lockable belt around the corseted line of her waist. The restraint settled her neatly into place beside him, not harshly, but with unmistakable intention. Once he had pocketed the key, he adjusted the small screen so she could read it without straining against her collar.
Anna sat very still for a moment, testing the arrangement through the body rather than thought.
&ldquo;Practical,&rdquo; she said, testing her voice after being mostly masked for weeks.
Julien glanced at the brochure tucked into the side compartment.
&ldquo;And approved. During travel, a guardian must keep his ward either escorted or secured.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes brightened behind the veil.
&ldquo;That sounds restful.&rdquo;
&ldquo;For whom?&rdquo;
Her hands moved in a smaller, prettier line this time.
&ldquo;Both, if managed well.&rdquo;
He touched the back of one gloved hand in agreement.
The train pulled away.
Fields blurred, then towns, then the wide industrial edges of movement between countries. Anna loosened the outer veil once they were underway and settled the hat more comfortably against the headrest. Without the heavier layering over her face, she looked younger and somehow more serious at once.
Julien read the travel notes while she read the carriage instructions. The British system had thought of everything, including bodily consequences. Integrated dock points for women traveling in full chastity belts. Guidance on hydration. Privacy protocols. Recommendations for layered rain gear and outdoor escort systems on arrival. The whole thing was so matter-of-fact that Anna felt the same dangerous pleasure she always felt when dependence was treated as a solved civic problem instead of a private shame.
She pointed toward the brochure in his hand.
&ldquo;They plan beautifully here.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You approve?&rdquo;
&ldquo;I am impressed.&rdquo;
He watched her for a second.
&ldquo;That is not the same thing.&rdquo;
Anna lowered her gaze in acknowledgment and then signed more carefully:
&ldquo;I believe I may approve when I know more.&rdquo;
That answer pleased him.
She spent part of the journey following the route display and part of it imagining London in layers: wet streets, guarded women under capes, rail systems tracing roofs and interiors, the practical shamelessness of British restraint. Beneath the image of the city was the more private anticipation she did not need to tell because Julien could already read it in her.
This was their taking-possession journey.
The phrase did not embarrass her anymore, not even in her own head.
At one point Julien folded the brochure, set it aside, and took her hand.
&ldquo;You are thinking too loudly.&rdquo;
Anna&rsquo;s eyes smiled behind the veil.
&ldquo;Forgive me, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
He turned her gloved hand once in his own.
&ldquo;I like when you look forward to things.&rdquo;
The words entered her body with familiar force.
She signed more slowly then, because the restraint, the privacy, and his attention made precision feel intimate.
&ldquo;I am looking forward to London, Sir. I am looking forward to being shown what suits me there.&rdquo;
His gaze sharpened.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
That one word settled her for the rest of the journey.
By the time the train carried them on toward Paris and the next leg after it, Anna had stopped thinking of London as a destination and started thinking of it as an extension Julien&rsquo;s guidance so far: a wetter, stranger, more visibly organized version of the life she had already chosen, waiting to close around them both.

Exploring London# London Zoo gave Anna exactly what she had hoped for and slightly more weather than she would have chosen.
Julien dressed her for the outing with the seriousness of a man preparing a woman not only for a city but for the city under rain. The black catsuit first, cool and close over her skin. Then the medium-thick rubber stockings and heeled boots practical enough for distance and elegant enough not to look apologetic. Over that, a cream latex blouse with enough head movement to count as relative indulgence by British standards, then one of the latex walking skirts held out by petticoats and still restrictive enough to make each step a composed little problem. Last came the lockable muff, the cape, the veiled hat, and the rail attachment at the back of her corset.
When he finished fastening the outfit, Anna stood in the bedroom and let the whole thing settle around her.
This was not Geneva dressing. Geneva made a woman proper. London made her operable under conditions.
Julien clipped her to the rail, then unfastened her and transferred the attachment to the outdoor line as naturally as if he were helping her with a coat.
&ldquo;Ready?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Very, Sir.&rdquo;
The walk through the city taught her more than the boutique had.
Rain on London did not interrupt the Changed order. It revealed its engineering. Women moved under capes whose restraint was disguised as weatherproofing, hands hidden, waists belted, bodies attached to escort lines or household rails. Men managed umbrellas, pace, crossings, and hardware with bored competence. No one behaved as though any of it required explanation.
Anna adored that.
At the zoo, the paths were softer underfoot than she expected, laid in weather-conscious materials that drained cleanly and gave back a faint greenish light in shaded sections. The rain came and went in intervals. Julien adjusted her cape, checked the rail linkage, unlocked and relocked the muff when required, and generally made himself the answer to every inconvenience her outfit created.
That, more than the outfit itself, put her in a state of almost dangerous contentment.
&ldquo;It is beautiful here.&rdquo;
&ldquo;The zoo or the system?&rdquo;
Her eyes smiled behind the veil.
&ldquo;Both.&rdquo;
The redesigned zoo pleased her on its own merits too. The old cage logic had been replaced with larger habitats, softer boundaries, green engineering, and a kind of environmental humility she found deeply satisfying. The paths curved around water systems and plantings designed not merely for public appearance but for climate management. The barriers were subtle enough to force a visitor to look for them. The animals, where visible, looked less exhibited than tolerated.
Anna tried to explain part of it to Julien while they walked.
&ldquo;They are not showing nature as ornament, but trying to leave enough room for function. That seems&hellip; British in the current sense.&rdquo;
Julien watched her through the veil, then the habitat beyond her.
&ldquo;Control hidden inside utility.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He looked at her for a second and then added, &ldquo;You do like that.&rdquo;
The answer took no time at all.
&ldquo;Very much.&rdquo;
They found a sheltered place for a snack when the rain thickened again.
Julien adjusted the rail position so she could kneel without strain, then unfastened the cape enough to get at the muff. Once her hands were freed, Anna had to manage the veil, the fastenings at the lower edge, the angle of her own head, and the need to eat without disarranging the rest of herself. It should have been absurd. Instead the small ceremony of it pleased her. Every bite required permission from the outfit and assistance from Julien. Every swallow reminded her how thoroughly the city expected a woman to be prepared by another person before she could move simply through the day.
Julien handed her the wrapped biscuit in two pieces to make it easier.
&ldquo;Small bites.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
When she finished, he waited while she refastened herself and closed the cape with a smoothness that felt practiced already. Julien then locked the muff again.
Anna looked down at her own body, reassembled for public use.
&ldquo;Thank you, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;For what part?&rdquo;
She thought about that.
&ldquo;For making it all seem simple.&rdquo;
That pleased him enough to show in the line of his mouth.
The walk back through Regent&rsquo;s Park took them past rain gardens, low-lit pathways, and plantings designed to drink the weather rather than fight it. Anna would have admired them in any case. Admiring them while clipped lightly to Julien, half-restrained by cape and muff, and moving through the rain in an outfit that made every step careful turned the whole afternoon into a kind of argument she was already predisposed to lose.
Restriction, here, did not apologize for itself. It solved problems. It made women manageable. It made men necessary. It did all of that while remaining elegant enough to feel like taste.
By the time they returned to the B&amp;B, Anna was wet only at the edges and deeply, unreasonably pleased.
Julien unfastened the outdoor attachment, freed her from the cape, then from the muff, and finally let her stand in the suite still dressed in catsuit, skirt, and blouse while the rain ran silver against the window behind her.
&ldquo;Well?&rdquo; he asked.
&ldquo;I understand London better now.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And?&rdquo;
She lowered her eyes with an honesty that was almost indecent.
&ldquo;I think it understands me too.&rdquo;

Maid service# By the time they finished unloading the equipment at Henry&rsquo;s villa, Sonia should have gone home.
That was the sensible version of the evening. Thank him, leave, take a shower, sit alone with the afterimage of the hood and the chains and try to decide what any of it meant before seeing him again.
Instead she heard herself say, &ldquo;If you are not in a hurry, Sir, would you let me cook for you?&rdquo;
Henry looked at her for one measured second.
&ldquo;At your place?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;All right.&rdquo;
The drive across Geneva felt stranger than the mountain road had. Less dramatic, more intimate. They were no longer heading toward an agreed experiment. They were carrying its consequences back into ordinary streets.
Once inside her apartment, Sonia asked him to wait in the sitting room and went to change. She opened the wardrobe, looked at her work clothes, looked at the maid dress folded behind them, and understood before she touched it that this was not costume. It was hunger.
She put it on anyway.
The black dress closed around her with the familiar firmness of a role that knew exactly what it wanted from her. White apron. Collar. Gloves. Work heels, if such a thing existed. By the time she tied the last bow, her breathing had steadied. The turmoil from the drive had narrowed into something usable.
When she entered the sitting room, Henry was standing near the bookshelf, looking at a framed photograph on the mantel.
He turned.
The silence that followed was not surprise. It was recognition.
Sonia lowered her gaze and curtsied.
&ldquo;If it pleases you, Sir, your maid would like to serve supper.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Would she.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He set the frame down carefully.
&ldquo;Then serve.&rdquo;
That one word settled her more effectively than any reassurance could have done.
She cooked something simple: pasta, butter, herbs, a little lemon, anchovies worked down into the pan until they became depth rather than fish. Henry sat where he could watch her. Sonia was aware of him every second, not in a nervous way, but in the way a compass is aware of north. She moved more quietly than usual, more efficiently too, because the role simplified the work. Chop, stir, plate, wipe, carry. No spare motion. No spare self.
When she set the plate before him, she asked, &ldquo;May this humble maid stand by, Sir?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
She remained beside the table with her hands folded, head slightly bowed, and felt the old Shanghai discipline return with a force that should have alarmed her more than it did. The difference was that this time she was not doing it for cover. She was doing it because being useful to Henry made the room feel arranged correctly.
He ate. She watched without staring.
&ldquo;Take your place,&rdquo; he said at one point.
Sonia looked up before she could stop herself.
&ldquo;Sir?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Not at the table. There.&rdquo;
He touched the rug beside his chair with two fingers.
She went down at once, knees together, back straight. The position was familiar enough to make her chest tighten. Henry resumed eating as if a woman at his knee were the most ordinary extension of supper in the world.
The peace of it hit her so suddenly she almost resented it.
&ldquo;You enjoy this,&rdquo; he said.
It was not a question.
Sonia kept her eyes lowered.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;How much?&rdquo;
That answer took longer.
&ldquo;Enough that it frightens me a little.&rdquo;
He finished the mouthful he was on before answering.
&ldquo;Fear means you are paying attention.&rdquo;
The line should have irritated her. Instead it relieved her, because it let the fear remain inside the room without forcing it to become a crisis.
When he finished eating, she cleared the table, washed the dishes, wiped down the counter, and restored the kitchen with the satisfaction of a person allowed to do the work she had been craving all evening. Henry did not praise every motion, he let the role stand. He expected it to continue.
When the apartment was in order, he called her back into the sitting room.
&ldquo;Come here.&rdquo;
She crossed to him and curtsied again.
&ldquo;What else does my maid want tonight?&rdquo;
The question went through her like a key turning.
She could have answered politely. Could have said whatever pleased you, Sir, and left it at that. Instead the truth came out before she could polish it.
&ldquo;To be useful. To be looked after. To stay like this a little longer.&rdquo;
Henry sat back, studying her.
&ldquo;Then kneel between my knees.&rdquo;
She obeyed.
Once there, close enough to feel the heat of him, Sonia understood that the maid role had carried her much further than an outfit should have been able to. She was not playacting service. She was inside it. Her body had already organized itself around the logic of command, usefulness, attention, approval.
Henry rested one hand lightly at the side of her neck.
&ldquo;Look at me.&rdquo;
She did.
&ldquo;You can still stop.&rdquo;
That offer mattered. She knew it mattered. It also made stopping feel less possible rather than more.
&ldquo;I do not want to stop, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No?&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, Sir.&rdquo;
He let his thumb brush once along the line beneath her jaw, not soft enough to be casual and not hard enough to count as force, just enough to keep her exactly where he wanted her while he watched her answer him.
&ldquo;Then serve dessert.&rdquo;
She breathed out, slow and shaky.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
The act itself was less important than what it did to her. Sonia had expected explicitness to break the spell, to return her to herself in some sharp embarrassed way. Instead it carried the service deeper. She unfastened him, took him into her mouth, and found that the same clean narrowing from the shoot could happen here too: task, body, praise anticipated. His hand in her hair. His voice above her. Her own pleasure threaded through obedience so tightly she could not separate them without lying.
At first she tried to tease him, hold him just at the edge, stay in control of the pace because part of her still wanted proof that she had not vanished into the role entirely.
Henry felt it at once.
His fingers tightened just enough at the back of her head.
&ldquo;No games now,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Finish properly.&rdquo;
The command landed as heat low in her body.
She obeyed with a devotion that shocked her.
When he came, when she stayed where he held her and took what he gave, the relief was not merely sexual. It was structural. She had been told what to do and had done it well. Her whole body lit around that fact.
She sat back on her heels afterward, breathing carefully, hands on her thighs, eyes down.
&ldquo;Thank you for using your maid, Sir.&rdquo;
Henry&rsquo;s hand came under her chin and lifted her face.
&ldquo;Good girl.&rdquo;
There it was.
Anna had tried to explain it once. Sonia had understood intellectually and dismissed half of it as viral softness.
This was not intellectual. The praise struck cleanly and far too deep, spreading through her in a hard bright rush that left her damp, weak, and abruptly ashamed of how much she wanted more of it.
Her forehead came to rest against his knee without decision.
He stroked her hair once.
&ldquo;Stay.&rdquo;
She stayed.
They did not rush into another explicit scene after that. That mattered. Henry let the praise and the service and the collapse stand on their own. Later he made tea. She served it. He asked for water. She brought it. The apartment slipped into a quieter version of the same order until it was too late to pretend the evening still belonged to ordinary categories.
When he rose to leave, Sonia surprised both of them by saying, &ldquo;Please don&rsquo;t.&rdquo;
Henry turned back.
She swallowed.
&ldquo;Sir.&rdquo;
He walked to her slowly.
&ldquo;You want me to stay?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
The answer was humiliating. It was also the most honest thing she had said all day.
He looked at the maid dress, the careful room, the strain she was putting into standing upright.
&ldquo;All right,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;I will stay. But you are not sleeping alone in that state.&rdquo;
She almost said thank you and stopped herself only because the gratitude was too large for the sentence.
Henry made her a nest of cushions and blankets at the foot of her bed. He would have let her undress, she thought, if she had asked. She did not ask. Remaining corseted, remaining in the maid dress, remaining arranged under his supervision felt less like deprivation than like continuity.
&ldquo;Lie down,&rdquo; he said.
She did.
&ldquo;Blanket.&rdquo;
She drew it over herself.
&ldquo;Wake me at seven.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
He slept in her bed while she slept at its foot and somehow that did not feel insulting. It felt exactly as if things were as they should be. She drifted in and out with the deep tiredness that follows a drop too complete to understand yet. The corset pressed at every breath. The memory of his praise stayed warm in her body long after the physical arousal had ebbed.
At dawn she woke before the alarm, washed, refreshed herself, and put the room back into order. Then she woke him with her mouth because the thought had come to her while she was laying out breakfast and once it arrived it had felt less like an invention than like the obvious correct way to begin his day.
He woke with a hand in her hair and a low sound she felt more than heard.
Later, breakfast was on the table, the cushions were stacked away, and Sonia knelt before him on the rug while sunlight moved gradually across her floorboards.
&ldquo;Talk to me,&rdquo; he said.
The first person came back more slowly than she expected.
&ldquo;About what, Sir?&rdquo;
&ldquo;About yesterday. About this.&rdquo;
She looked at her own hands in her lap.
&ldquo;I thought the maid role would feel like an indulgence.&rdquo; She paused. &ldquo;It does not. It feels like a door. When I go through it, I stop wasting energy on the parts of myself that want to argue for the sake of arguing.&rdquo;
Henry said nothing. That helped.
&ldquo;In Shanghai I found it comforting and hated that I found it comforting. Yesterday with you&hellip;&rdquo; She stopped and started again. &ldquo;Yesterday with you it went further than comfort. I wanted the role. I wanted the commands. I wanted to make myself useful and stay there.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And the praise?&rdquo;
Her face heated.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;What about it?&rdquo;
She forced herself to answer directly.
&ldquo;It felt like something opening.&rdquo; Her voice dropped. &ldquo;Something I have been standing in front of for a long time.&rdquo;
Henry leaned forward slightly.
&ldquo;Do you think this is a game?&rdquo;
&ldquo;No, Sir.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Do you think it is the virus?&rdquo;
That was the old emergency exit. She heard it and refused it.
&ldquo;No.&rdquo; Sonia lifted her eyes to him for the first time in several sentences. &ldquo;I think the virus made the world easier to read. I think Shanghai showed me something. I think yesterday proved I want it. But wanting it is mine.&rdquo;
He held her gaze for a moment, then nodded.
&ldquo;Very well.&rdquo;
The words warmed her again.
&ldquo;Because if you continue with me, Sonia, I am not interested in a decorative submission you wear on weekends and analyze to death afterward. I want something lived.&rdquo;
She drew in a careful breath.
&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Do you.&rdquo;
She thought of the maid dress on her body, the rug under her knees, the peace she had felt with his plate in her hands, the humiliating purity of good girl, the calm of sleeping at his feet because he had told her to.
&ldquo;Yes, Sir,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;I do.&rdquo;
By the time he left that morning, Sonia was still not ready to name what had changed. She only knew that the maid role no longer felt like cover, or experiment, or even kink in any limited sense. It felt like an access point into a life where belonging to someone in the right way might make her more free, not less.

Three years earlier: The End of Delacroix and Partners# Mara Delacroix sold her firm on a Tuesday and spent the afternoon correcting the men who had bought it from her.
That was the insult of it. Not that Delacroix &amp; Partners would become Renard and Girard. Not even that her name would come off the glass by the end of the month. The insult was that she could still see more clearly than either of them and could no longer trust herself to live at that pitch without the Change grinding against her from inside.
Paul Renard was halfway through a client note when Mara reached across his desk, took the marked draft from his hand, and crossed out three lines.
&ldquo;If you send this,&rdquo; she said, &ldquo;the counterparty will hear apology where you meant caution.&rdquo;
Paul watched her with the careful face men used now when a woman was useful and slightly unsettling.
&ldquo;You no longer have to do this,&rdquo; he said.
&ldquo;Evidently I do.&rdquo;
She rewrote the paragraph, set the page down, and felt the familiar recoil start under her ribs. The sentence had been sharp. Clean. Necessary. It should have satisfied her. Instead the pressure came again, that ugly internal drag that had been getting worse for months: not guilt, not fear, something more invasive. A resistance to her own force. A sense that each hard push cost more than it should.
She kept her face still until it passed.
Antoine Girard appeared in the doorway a few minutes later with that false-soft manner he reserved for nervous clients and injured animals.
&ldquo;Mara. We hoped to speak.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You are speaking.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Privately.&rdquo;
She let them follow her into her office because refusing would only lengthen the scene.
The room still looked like hers. The framed degrees. The lacquered shelves. The city map with its colored pins. Her name in brass on the inner door. By next week all of it would be paperwork or memory.
Paul shut the door.
&ldquo;We wanted to thank you properly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Do it quickly.&rdquo;
They exchanged a glance.
Antoine said, &ldquo;The firm exists because of you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;The firm still exists because I sold it before I tore into the wrong man in the wrong room and made us all a spectacle.&rdquo;
&ldquo;That is not fair,&rdquo; Paul said.
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; Mara said. &ldquo;It is precise.&rdquo;
The pressure flared again. Too blunt. Too aggressive. Some part of her now hated the exact qualities that had built her career. She felt it each time she cut, each time she dominated, each time she used certainty like a weapon. The old reflex rose; the Change pushed back; her body paid for both.
Paul took a step nearer.
&ldquo;You are not a spectacle.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I would have been.&rdquo; She looked from one of them to the other. &ldquo;You have both seen it. The hesitation. The drag. The way conflict catches now. I can still win a room. I cannot stay in one all day without feeling the cost.&rdquo;
That, finally, shut them up.
Mara dismissed them with a glance. When the door closed, she sat very still in her chair and looked at the legal pad on her desk.
At the top of the page, in her own neat hand, was a heading she despised:
Potential guardians.
She had made the list the way she did everything important. Coldly. Comparative strengths. Weaknesses. Likely appetites. Likely vanities. Men to avoid at any cost. Men who would enjoy owning a difficult woman. Men too stupid to manage one. Men who would mistake possession for leadership and become cruel when that failed to flatter them.
At the bottom sat the only name she had not crossed out.
Philippe Beaumont.
Former diplomat. Widower. Old enough not to perform masculinity like a boy showing off a weapon. Precise. Social. Annoyingly perceptive. A man who had once taken a room away from her in Brussels by letting everyone else think the compromise had been their own idea.
She had hated him for that. She had respected him for it more.
Mara picked up her phone, set it down again, then forced herself to dial before she could spend another hour pretending there was some nobler option still waiting to emerge.
He answered on the second ring.
&ldquo;Mara Delacroix. It has been a long time.&rdquo;
His voice was familiar and warm enough to be dangerous.
&ldquo;It has,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;How have you been, Philippe?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Busy, as always. I survive. And you?&rdquo;
Mara crossed to the window before answering. Below her, the city moved through its new order with the unnerving composure of a place already forgetting it had ever been arranged differently.
&ldquo;Managing,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;It has been a difficult season.&rdquo;
His voice softened.
&ldquo;I have heard enough to know the Change has not been kind to people like you.&rdquo;
That almost made her laugh.
&ldquo;People like me.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sharp women with no interest in becoming decorative.&rdquo;
There it was. One reason she had called him and not one of the smoother, safer names on the list.
&ldquo;I sold the firm this morning,&rdquo; she said.
He was silent for a moment.
&ldquo;I am sorry.&rdquo;
&ldquo;It was necessary.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I assume you did not do it lightly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
She rested one hand against the glass.
&ldquo;The pressure had become too constant. I could still work. I could not go on working in the same way.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Delacroix &amp; Partners was never only work to you.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
The quiet on the line turned gentler. Not pitying. Attentive.
&ldquo;What comes next?&rdquo; he asked.
That was the opening she had been circling from the moment she picked up the phone.
&ldquo;That is why I am calling.&rdquo;
The words caught more than she wanted them to. The Change had been pushing at her speech for weeks now, trying to soften it, lower it, redirect it into newer forms of female deference that she refused to grant a place this early in the conversation. She held onto the older grammar by force.
&ldquo;The social expectations for women have changed,&rdquo; she said carefully. &ldquo;You know that as well as I do.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I do.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And it has become&hellip;&rdquo; She hated the pause. &ldquo;Prudent to make arrangements before one is forced into them badly.&rdquo;
He did not interrupt.
&ldquo;I have been considering who might be suitable,&rdquo; Mara said. &ldquo;Someone intelligent. Someone discreet. Someone I could trust not to make a spectacle out of necessity.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And you have come to a decision.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I have.&rdquo;
She drew a breath and made herself say it plainly.
&ldquo;I would ask you to take me as your ward.&rdquo;
Another silence. Not shocked. Measuring.
&ldquo;You are not a woman who says something like that lightly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And what, exactly, do you imagine the arrangement would be?&rdquo;
Now she was back on firmer ground. Terms. Scope. Containment.
&ldquo;Formal. Respectable. Sufficient to satisfy what society now expects. I remain useful. You act as guardian in name. We behave with discretion.&rdquo;
He breathed out softly. Not quite a sigh.
&ldquo;Mara.&rdquo;
She closed her eyes.
&ldquo;You are proposing a practical shelter from the weather,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;That may not be all this turns out to be.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I am proposing what is necessary.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Perhaps. But perhaps necessity now means more than a title and an address.&rdquo;
She stiffened.
&ldquo;I am not asking for sentiment.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Nor am I offering an answer on the phone.&rdquo;
That stopped her.
His tone stayed warm. Firm underneath it.
&ldquo;Come tomorrow to Champel. We will talk properly. If, after that, you still think a guardian only in name is enough, you may tell me so to my face.&rdquo;
Mara bristled immediately.
&ldquo;Very well.&rdquo;
&ldquo;One more thing.&rdquo;
&ldquo;What.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Do not rehearse too much tonight.&rdquo;
The line was so exactly aimed that she almost hated him for it already.
&ldquo;You always build an opening position before anyone can touch the real question,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;It was effective in Brussels. It will not help you much here.&rdquo;
He ended the call before she could score anything off him.
Mara stood in her office with the dead phone in her hand and a sudden violent wish to throw it through the window. Instead she set it down, gathered the last folders she still intended to review, and kept working until the building emptied around her.
That night she changed into practice clothes and used movement to stop herself from thinking.
No music. No mirror. Just the living room floor and the old grammar of her body: turnout, stretch, extension, balance, hold. Ballet had taught her young that pain could be sorted, absorbed, used. It had also taught her that control lived in repetition long before it lived in comfort.
She moved until sweat cooled on her back and the city lights outside had gone fully blue.
Then the phrases she had meant to use on Philippe began to sound false even in her own head.
I remain useful. I require discretion. This need not become intimate.
None of that was the real question.
The real question crouched underneath it all, offensive and persistent.
Could the right man make this bearable?
Protection had begun appearing in her thoughts without permission. So had care. So had the far worse possibility that being held inside a structure chosen in time might feel less degrading than going on alone until the Change chose one for her by force or exhaustion.
She sank into a long stretch and stayed there because anger needed somewhere to go.
&ldquo;Do not lie to me,&rdquo; she said aloud to the empty room.
That, at least, still sounded like Mara.
The next morning she stood before her wardrobe longer than necessary.
She could still dress like the old Mara if she wished. She had the jackets, the sharper cuts, the heels that announced impact. Yet even that calculation had changed. The more aggressive lines now looked strained, like legal arguments made after the court had already turned.
In the end she chose dark silk, a fitted jacket, a skirt with enough shape to read woman before weapon, and heels that made no pretense of comfort. She pinned her hair, put on lipstick, looked at herself once, and saw compromise where she would once have seen strategy.
Then she took her bag, locked the apartment, and went to Champel.
Philippe&rsquo;s villa sat above the lake with the composure of a house built by someone who believed that control should never need to raise its voice.
She met Philippe in his study opening onto the garden. The room had changed less than the city had. Good furniture. Books. Diplomatic prints. Fresh flowers placed by a woman who had either excellent taste or good instructions.
Philippe led her there, wearing a dark suit, silver at the temples, every inch as composed as she remembered.
&ldquo;Mara.&rdquo;
He did not offer his hand. That was wise.
&ldquo;Philippe.&rdquo;
He indicated the sofa.
&ldquo;Sit.&rdquo;
Not please. Not would you like. A small thing. Not small at all.
Mara sat.
Tea arrived, placed and withdrawn with the same smooth efficiency as everything else in the house. Philippe waited until they were alone again before speaking.
&ldquo;You have had the night to reconsider.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I have had the night to refine.&rdquo;
That almost drew a smile from him.
&ldquo;Of course you have.&rdquo;
He sat opposite her, not close enough to crowd, not far enough to make the conversation feel abstract.
&ldquo;Then let us be precise,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Tell me what you think you are asking for.&rdquo;
Mara had prepared this part.
&ldquo;A formal guardianship. Respectable, discreet, and administratively clear. I continue to work where I can be useful. You provide social cover, legal standing, and household affiliation. We avoid melodrama.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And in private?&rdquo;
She held his gaze.
&ldquo;In private I remain myself.&rdquo;
&ldquo;You believe that can be separated so neatly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I believe it must be.&rdquo;
Philippe folded one leg over the other.
&ldquo;No,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;That is the part you still have wrong.&rdquo;
The refusal was calm. Worse than anger for that reason.
&ldquo;Explain.&rdquo;
&ldquo;A guardian only in name is not stable. Not for society, and not for you.&rdquo; He did not soften the pronoun. &ldquo;You are already under pressure from the Change. If the public form says one thing and the private life says another, you will be made to live in contradiction every day. That will not preserve you. It will tear you apart.&rdquo;
Mara&rsquo;s jaw tightened.
&ldquo;I am not asking to be indulged.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No. You are asking to be allowed to treat this as a paper structure.&rdquo; He leaned back slightly. &ldquo;I will not do that.&rdquo;
She looked out toward the garden for one measured second, then back.
&ldquo;What, then, would you do?&rdquo;
&ldquo;If I take you, I take you properly.&rdquo;
The line landed so directly that for a moment she only listened to her own pulse.
Philippe continued in the same composed tone.
&ldquo;You would live here. You would be answerable to me in matters of presentation, household conduct, and anything that affects us both. I would expect honesty before damage control. I would expect obedience, not constant negotiation disguised as professional language.&rdquo;
Mara almost interrupted at that and stopped herself.
He noticed.
&ldquo;You would still be useful,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;I have no interest in making you dull. But usefulness would no longer excuse autonomy in every domain.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And what do I receive in return?&rdquo; she asked.
The old instinct was still there, still putting terms on the table even now.
Philippe accepted that without mocking it.
&ldquo;Clarity. Protection. A house in which you are not asked to perform a role your own changed mind increasingly refuses to sustain. I do not lie. I do not humiliate for entertainment. I do not expect gratitude for necessities. If I set rules, they will be rules I mean to keep.&rdquo;
That was closer to what she wanted than anything he had said on the phone.
&ldquo;Work?&rdquo; she asked.
&ldquo;Yes, where it can be done properly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Intellectual work.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Discretion.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Public discretion, certainly. Private truth, also.&rdquo;
She took that in.
&ldquo;And if I find your rules impossible.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Then you should not come under my care.&rdquo;
No flourish. No attempt to persuade. Just the line itself.
It made the whole thing feel more serious and less escapable.
Mara set her teacup down untouched.
&ldquo;What rules.&rdquo;
Philippe reached to the table beside him, lifted a single sheet of paper, and handed it to her.
There were not many lines on it. Fewer than she had expected. That made them worse.
I will be truthful with Philippe.
I will inform him before acting in matters that affect us both.
I will obey his decisions in matters of my dress, conduct, and presentation.
I will not treat dependence as degradation.
I will ask rather than seize.
I will allow myself to be cared for.
Mara read them once, then again.
&ldquo;You prepared this.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Before I called?&rdquo;
&ldquo;No. After.&rdquo;
That, at least, was better.
She held the paper between both hands.
&ldquo;You understand that this is not equal.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Of course it is not equal,&rdquo; Philippe said. &ldquo;It is guardianship.&rdquo;
The bluntness of it should have offended her more than it did.
&ldquo;And yet you still came.&rdquo;
She hated how easily he could say that.
&ldquo;I came to negotiate.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Then negotiate.&rdquo;
Mara looked down at the page.
&ldquo;I will not be lied to,&rdquo; she said.
&ldquo;Agreed.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I will not be paraded as an idiot.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Agreed.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I will remain useful.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I will not be cut off from matters I am capable of understanding.&rdquo;
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And if you expect obedience, you do not hide that expectation under sentimental language.&rdquo;
That did bring the smallest flicker to his mouth.
&ldquo;Agreed.&rdquo;
She read the paper again.
The thing she had come to do was already happening, and not in the way she had imagined. She had thought she was arranging a shelter. Instead she was stepping across a line neither of them was pretending not to see.
&ldquo;You may take time,&rdquo; Philippe said. &ldquo;A day. Two. I would prefer it, in fact.&rdquo;
Mara lifted her head.
&ldquo;No.&rdquo;
He waited.
&ldquo;If I leave this room uncommitted, I will turn it back into theory. I know myself too well for that.&rdquo; She set the paper in her lap. &ldquo;I accept.&rdquo;
The words changed the room at once.
Not legally. Not yet.
Socially. Personally. Structurally.
She saw it in his face before he said anything. The shift from candidate to guardian. From one former equal deciding whether to help another, to a man who had just been accepted into authority over her.
Philippe rose.
&ldquo;Then stand.&rdquo;
Mara stood because of course she did.
&ldquo;Come here.&rdquo;
She crossed the room and stopped in front of him.
He was close enough now that she had to tip her head a little to keep his face in view.
&ldquo;The legal act can be completed this week,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;That is paperwork. This is not.&rdquo;
Her throat tightened.
&ldquo;You understand that from this moment you are, in every way that matters, Philippe Beaumont&rsquo;s Mara.&rdquo;
There it was.
She had known it already. Hearing it made it real in the body.
&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; she said.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
He took the paper from her hand and held it between them.
&ldquo;My first command to you is simple. You will say these rules back to me in the first person.&rdquo;
Mara stared at him.
&ldquo;Why.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Because I want you to hear your own voice carrying them. Because language accepted aloud settles differently. Because I am not interested in keeping you half outside this.&rdquo;
That was so infuriatingly reasoned that refusal would have sounded childish even to her.
Philippe touched the first line with one finger.
&ldquo;Begin.&rdquo;
Mara drew a breath that felt far too shallow.
&ldquo;I will be truthful with Philippe.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Again. Properly.&rdquo;
The correction was quiet and immediate. She felt it all the way down her spine.
&ldquo;I will be truthful with Philippe,&rdquo; she said, more steadily.
He moved to the next line.
&ldquo;I will inform him before acting in matters that affect us both.&rdquo;
The third line caught slightly in her throat.
&ldquo;I will obey his decisions in matters of my dress, conduct, and presentation.&rdquo;
She heard the asymmetry as she spoke it. Heard it and kept going.
&ldquo;I will not treat dependence as degradation.&rdquo;
That one hurt more than obedience had.
&ldquo;I will ask rather than seize.&rdquo;
Philippe&rsquo;s eyes did not leave her face.
&ldquo;And the last.&rdquo;
Mara looked at the words.
For one foolish second she almost laughed at the obscenity of how much they frightened her.
Then she said them anyway.
&ldquo;I will allow myself to be cared for.&rdquo;
Silence held after the final line.
Philippe folded the paper once and set it aside.
&ldquo;Good.&rdquo;
The word landed with far more force than praise had any right to carry.
&ldquo;Go home now,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Tomorrow you will return, and we will begin with the legal forms.&rdquo;
Mara should have answered with something cutting, something that put air back into the room.
Instead she heard herself say, &ldquo;Yes.&rdquo;
Not Sir. Not yet.
But when she turned and walked back through the house, past the quiet maid and the ordered flowers and the rooms that had suddenly become legible as the site of her future, she knew the line had already been crossed.
The signatures could wait.
She had entered the villa as Mara Delacroix evaluating a candidate. She left it as Philippe Beaumont&rsquo;s Mara.

Plans involving Art and Fashion# After the spectacle of the opera, the Freeborn strike again, kidnapping Sonia and Mara. Stripped of her iGuardian, Mara is thrown into a world of raw exposure: forced to walk, eat, and speak like an Unchanged, every moment without her mask and corset feels like filth and collapse. Sonia, locked at her side, remembers her own corrections under Henry—how the chastity belt that once tormented her became her badge of service and her peace. Together, they endure captivity until a desperate escape and their rescue by grenadiers. But survival is only the beginning: Mara can no longer imagine life without her shell, and when Philippe fits her with a new iGuardian Pro Secure, its discipline and rewards restore her to herself. Sonia, meanwhile, chooses her own submission, giving Henry her devotion with clarity. In a Changed world of masks, corsets, and ritual obedience, both women discover that harmony comes not by resisting, but by finding where love and discipline meet.
While their wards relaxed on the veranda, Philippe and Henry sat in the living room, enjoying classical music. Dressed in tailored suits, they sipped their drinks and talked about their love for the opera.
&ldquo;It’s been ages since I went to a performance,&rdquo; Henry said, a bit wistfully. &ldquo;Work&rsquo;s been nonstop, but an evening at the opera would be perfect.&rdquo;
Philippe nodded. &ldquo;There’s something timeless about it. The music, the drama, the elegance.&rdquo;
Henry’s eyes lit up. &ldquo;Have you seen Il Seraglio? It’s coming to the Grand Théâtre soon.&rdquo;
Philippe smiled. &ldquo;Il Seraglio is a great choice. The themes of captivity and freedom are powerful, and the setting is so rich and exotic.&rdquo;
Henry leaned forward, excited. &ldquo;It’s also a chance to dress up and enjoy high fashion. How about we dress our wards to match the theme of the opera?&rdquo;
Philippe chuckled. &ldquo;High fashion with a harem theme? That sounds like a fascinating mix of art and discipline. Mara could wear something that captures the essence of Il Seraglio.&rdquo;
Henry nodded, already imagining it. &ldquo;I can see Sonia in an outfit inspired by the seraglio, with plenty of veils and elements that symbolize captivity, yet elegant enough for the opera.&rdquo;
Philippe’s eyes sparkled. &ldquo;For Mara, something rich, maybe silk and brocade, adorned with jewels to show her status.&rdquo;
Henry added, &ldquo;And for Sonia, a gown with layers of sheer fabric, representing the veils of the harem, yet structured to ensure discipline and posture.&rdquo;
Philippe leaned back, picturing the scene. &ldquo;It would be a spectacle to see our wards dressed like that, capturing the essence of the opera. It’s settled then. We’ll attend Il Seraglio together in my box at the Grand Théâtre. There’s room for four, so it’s perfect.&rdquo;
Henry raised his glass. &ldquo;To opera and high fashion. And to the exquisite blend of art, discipline, and elegance that awaits us.&rdquo;
Philippe smiled and clinked his glass with Henry’s. &ldquo;To an unforgettable evening at the opera.&rdquo; As they discussed more details, their excitement grew, knowing this evening would perfectly blend their passions.
Sonia, dressed in her maid outfit, prepared breakfast for Henry. The white apron and gloves contrasted with her dark dress, and a corset tightly cinched her waist. Having served, she knelt down on her cushion beside Henry, assuming her usual pose—hands behind her back, chest forward, and bottom out. The corset and dress enforced her posture as she waited to be fed.
Henry sat down, smiling as he appreciated her submissive stance. &ldquo;Good morning, Sonia,&rdquo; he said, reaching for a piece of fruit. &ldquo;I received an email from Philippe this morning. We’ll be attending the opera next Saturday, Il Seraglio.&rdquo; He paused as Sonia accepted the bite.
Sonia&rsquo;s eyes lit up with excitement. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s wonderful news, Sir,&rdquo; she said, looking up at him. &ldquo;May I ask if you have plans for my attire? The theme seems perfectly aligned with our shared interests.&rdquo;
Henry nodded, feeding her another bite. &ldquo;I do have some ideas, Sonia. You will indeed be dressed to match the theme.&rdquo;
Sonia felt a thrill of anticipation. &ldquo;Sir, if it pleases you, may I inquire about my role during the evening? Will I be more ornamental or active?&rdquo; Her eyes were soft and pleading as she continued. &ldquo;I’ve grown to love the opera, Sir, and would greatly enjoy wearing a dress that allows me to hear and see the performance. I hope this aligns with your plans.&rdquo;
Henry looked down at her, thoughtful. &ldquo;I assure you, Sonia, I’ve considered your enjoyment. You will be ornamental, but I’ve designed something that will let you appreciate the performance fully.&rdquo;
Sonia&rsquo;s eyes sparkled with gratitude. &ldquo;Thank you, Sir. That means the world to me.&rdquo;
Henry smiled, feeding her another bite. &ldquo;You will look exquisite, Sonia. The dress will reflect the opulence and exoticism of Il Seraglio while ensuring you remain appropriately restrained and elegant.&rdquo;
Sonia knew Henry’s plans for her attire would be both challenging and beautiful.
&ldquo;Now,&rdquo; Henry continued, selecting the next bite for Sonia, &ldquo;let’s finish breakfast. We have much to prepare for the coming days.&rdquo;
At Philippe&rsquo;s home, Mara sat secured to her chair at the breakfast table. As usual, Philippe had connected her feed bag to her mask himself, gently delivering her meal. He then sat across from her, calm and composed as he began to speak.
&ldquo;Mara,&rdquo; Philippe said, breaking the silence. &ldquo;I have news. We will be attending the opera next Saturday, in ten days. We’ll meet Henry and Sonia there.&rdquo;
Mara&rsquo;s eyes brightened with excitement, and she signed her answer. This one looks forward to that, Sir. It will be wonderful to see Sonia again.
Philippe nodded with a smile. &ldquo;Indeed. The opera will be Il Seraglio.&rdquo;
Mara&rsquo;s excitement grew. Philippe continued, &ldquo;I’m thinking of dressing you as Konstanze, Mara. You’ll be veiled and chained, as befits a captive woman in a seraglio.&rdquo;
Mara’s heart sank slightly at the thought of being dressed in chains and without a mask. She wouldn’t be able to sign and would have to speak, which made her uneasy. She disliked using her voice. But she knew better than to directly express her discomfort.
Her fingers moved slowly, carefully signing. Sir, your wishes are understood, and of course this one will fulfill her assigned role. However, if it pleases you, she would feel more comfortable masked. Using her voice feels less modest. Hand-dancing is more expressive, and more feminine, she believes.
Philippe watched her closely, noticing the careful way she made her request. He considered her words, then nodded thoughtfully. &ldquo;I understand, Mara. If it makes you more comfortable, I will reconsider. You will still be chained, but I’ll ensure you can sign, though within limits.&rdquo;
Mara’s eyes softened with relief, and she signed her gratitude. Thank you, Sir, for your thoughtful consideration. This one is deeply grateful.
Philippe’s smile widened, pleased with her response. &ldquo;Good. You will look stunning as my Konstanze, and I’m sure you will perform admirably. Now, let’s finish our breakfast.&rdquo;
Mara folded her hands in her lap, feeling a deep sense of gratitude as she waited for the feed bag to empty while Philippe ate.
Saturday evening arrived with an air of anticipation and elegance. Philippe guided Mara out of the bathroom, where her iGuardian had just completed its cleaning cycle. The cleaning station released her from its locks, leaving her fresh and ready for the evening ahead. On the bed, the rich, dark red silk dress awaited her, each piece laid out, promising both opulence and confinement.
Philippe helped Mara sit on the edge of the bed. He started by gently removing her ballet boots, carefully unlocking the fasteners and sliding them off her feet. Next, he peeled off her stockings. Mara wasn&rsquo;t used to the cool air on her bare skin, but before she could feel uneasy, Philippe began to dress her for the evening.
He retrieved a pair of silk stockings, their material shimmering. Philippe slowly rolled them up her legs, smoothing out any wrinkles and ensuring they fit snugly. Once the stockings were clipped to the garters of the iGuardian, he reached for the high-heeled knee boots, matching the deep red of her dress and adorned with small gold buckles. He laced them up carefully, making sure each tie was secure before locking the buckles in place.
He led Mara to her vanity, locking her in place so she could apply her makeup and fix her hair while he tended to his own attire. Mara powdered her hair lightly, arranging it into a high, elegant updo. She interspersed the powdered locks with delicate flowers and threads of gold, each piece placed to create a look of aristocratic elegance. Then she laid out her final touches: a pearl necklace and matching earrings, and waited for Philippe to return, unlock her, and finish dressing her.
Philippe, dressed in a distinguished midnight blue velvet tailcoat with gold-embroidered lapels and embossed gold buttons, completed his own preparations. Draped diagonally across his chest was a crimson silk sash adorned with miniature medals and honors. His crisp white silk shirt featured a high stiff collar and a deep crimson tie, perfectly complementing the ensemble. Slim-cut trousers with a subtle gold braid on the outer seam and polished black patent leather Oxfords completed the look. A golden brooch of the Royal Coat of Arms and a black opera cloak lined with crimson silk added the final touches of refined elegance.
He then turned his attention back to Mara. He slipped her hands into the red velvet opera gloves, embroidered with intricate gold designs. The gloves reached up to her shoulders, where he fastened them to the braces of the iGuardian, ensuring they stayed in place throughout the evening.
Unlocking her, Philippe then helped Mara to her feet to guide her into her numerous skirts. The lowest layer was her smooth and narrow Yoyah Sheengzoh, tightly encasing her legs and restricting her step. He layered several petticoats over it, adding volume and structure to her silhouette. The richly decorated and heavy skirt followed, its weight settling around her hips as he adjusted the fabric.
Next, Philippe picked up the stiff embroidered bodice. He carefully positioned it on Mara, making sure it aligned perfectly with the skirt before buttoning it up the back. The final button was locked into place, securing the bodice to the skirt and ensuring there would be no shifting or loosening as the evening progressed.
With the main parts of her dress in place, Philippe then reached for the mask. The mask was a work of art, intricately painted with swirling patterns of gold and crimson, designed to match her dress. It featured a demanding gag, crafted to fit her mouth precisely. Philippe carefully positioned it over Mara’s face, then began the process of working the gag into her mouth. He had to shift it back and forth slightly, ensuring it settled correctly, until Mara could bite down on it. Once it was secure, Philippe clicked the mask to her collar, fixing her head in place. A thin chiffon veil was attached to the mask, insubstantial and semi-transparent, more of an elegant afterthought that allowed the beauty of the mask beneath to show through.
Finally, Philippe took a set of heavy manacles and clasped them around Mara’s wrists. The chains between them were long enough to allow her to sign, though they restricted her movements, preventing her from lifting her hands above elbow level. He fastened the chain to a hardpoint on the iGuardian through a small front opening in her dress, ensuring it was secure while still giving her some freedom to communicate.
Mara stood before Philippe, transformed into Konstanze, a vision of restrained elegance and beauty, ready for the opera.

Intermission# When Mara said, &ldquo;Love is shown through the discipline that shapes us into our true selves,&rdquo; Sonia’s thoughts drifted back to her early days with Henry. She remembered how his guidance had helped her become the person she wanted to be. She had given herself to him completely, and he had fully embraced her in return. Sonia smiled under her veil, recalling how the chastity belt no longer stayed locked once they fully embraced their unequal relationship. As Mara had once said, &ldquo;A ward silently enjoys what her guardian offers.&rdquo;
As the chaos subsided, Philippe checked outside. When it seemed safe, they cautiously returned to the foyer where they met with Henry. As quickly as her chained legs allowed, Sonia glided over to him and leaned into his embrace. The only evidence of the disturbance was broken glass, as Directeur Perroux addressed the guests, assuring them that everything was under control and the show would go on.
Mara processed the events, realizing how quickly Philippe and Henry had prioritized their wards&rsquo; safety. She felt a deep sense of gratitude and admiration for Philippe&rsquo;s protective instincts. Glancing at Sonia, who seemed equally affected, their eyes met, sharing a mutual sense of relief and thankfulness for their guardians&rsquo; quick actions.
&ldquo;Are you alright, Sonia?&rdquo; Henry asked gently.
Sonia curtsied and answered, &ldquo;Yes, Sir. Thank you for protecting us.&rdquo;
Philippe reassured Mara, and they all returned to their seats. As the second act began, Mara leaned closer to Philippe, feeling an even deeper connection. She was his ward, and he was her guardian—her anchor in a world of uncertainty.
Meanwhile, on stage, Blondchen began to reject Osmin&rsquo;s rude advances in her aria &ldquo;Durch Zärtlichkeit und Schmeicheln.&rdquo; Mara smiled behind her mask, thinking, &ldquo;If Blondchen only knew…&rdquo;
After the finale, Philippe secured a private VIP box at the Carré d’Or. He ordered red wine and Turkish delight for Henry, Sonia, and himself, while the women, securely locked to their seats, were unchained. As they were being served, Sonia noticed something unusual. The waitress, named-tagged as Janice on her uniform, wore a discreet &ldquo;S&rdquo; pin pinned to her dress. Sonia knew what that meant—Janice was really a man, an abusive guardian serving a year and a day–a Shadow year, hence the S–as a ward to learn proper restraint and gain a new perspective.
Sonia discreetly signed to Mara. Did Mara notice the pin? Before Mara could respond, Henry caught sight of Sonia’s gossiping. His expression hardened. This was the second time he caught Sonia signing about other wards corrections behind his back. He did not know why she was obsessed with that, but he could certainly oblige once they were back at home.
&ldquo;Sonia,&rdquo; Henry said firmly, in a low but clear voice, &ldquo;stop that. I thought I had made myself clear. Gossiping about corrections is unacceptable. Corrections are private matters, meant to guide and help you grow. Once they’re done, they are not spoken of again, especially not in public. For all practical purposes, Janice is a ward, and she is subject to exactly the same expectations and standards as any other ward.&rdquo;
Sonia immediately lowered her gaze and signed with full formality. This one apologizes, Sir. Thank you for keeping her aligned.
Henry nodded, his expression softening slightly. &ldquo;See that you remember this, Sonia. We will discuss it further at home.&rdquo; Sonia turned her attention to the Turkish delight and the wine, wondering what, exactly, that &ldquo;discussion&rdquo; at home would involve. Meanwhile, Janice returned with a feed bag for Mara, which Philippe attached to her feed port himself.
The conversation between the men turned to the Freeborn attack, leaving the women to themselves.
&ldquo;It seems too convenient that this attack happened just as the new Swiss banking transparency laws are coming into effect,&rdquo; Philippe remarked. &ldquo;These laws will expose many hidden fortunes in previously anonymous Swiss accounts and vaults, subjecting them to proper wealth taxes. The timing is suspicious.&rdquo;
Henry nodded but offered a different perspective. &ldquo;I see your point, Philippe, but we must consider all possibilities. It&rsquo;s possible that Chinese-sponsored terror groups could be fueling these masked attacks. They have their own reasons for destabilizing the region, especially with their increasing influence in global affairs.&rdquo;
Sonia wisely remained silent. She was cautious, understanding the delicate balance of their roles, and refrained from interrupting her already upset guardian. Besides, her knowledge of the true origin of the LASS-VASH-2024 was secret and maybe not even relevant in their men&rsquo;s discussion.
The men debated various motivations behind the attack, and eventually, it was time to leave. The men re-secured their wards and led them to the waiting limousine.
At home, Mara thanked Philippe for the wonderful evening and the opera. She signed with genuine gratitude. Thank you, Sir. It was a wonderful experience.
Philippe smiled, pleased, and helped her prepare for the night, locking her to the bed in her usual attire. Mara felt a sense of peace and safety as Philippe ensured every detail was just right. As she lay down, feeling the familiar constraints of her night setup, she felt happy and protected. With a contented sigh, she closed her eyes and drifted into a deep, restful sleep.
